   9:  * "five", because the count is not the property that matters: what matters is that a rule has one
  10:  * home.)
  11:  *
  12:  * WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL, because the alternative is what most acceptance suites do and it is
  13:  * worth naming: `adapterDerivedCapability()` in `tests/at/harness/capabilities.ts` stamps every
  14:  * `sut.<key>` a STAND-IN unconditionally, so a suite whose fixture adapter re-implements the
  15:  * requirement proves that the TEST is well-formed and nothing whatever about the code that ships.
  16:  * `tests/at/suites/req-016/_fixture.ts` says so about itself in its own opening paragraph. Putting
  17:  * the judgements here and importing them from both sides is what makes the loop-tier green a claim
  18:  * about product code. The adapter still supplies storage — that half is a stand-in and stays one.
  19:  *
  20:  * TWO CONSTRAINTS THIS FILE IS UNDER, and both are real rather than stylistic:
  21:  *
  22:  *   1. ZERO NON-RELATIVE IMPORTS AND NO DENO GLOBAL. It is compiled by `tests/at/tsconfig.json`,
  23:  *      which is `strict` with `skipLibCheck: false`, `types: ["node"]` and no DOM library — a bare
  24:  *      `jsr:`/`npm:` specifier or a reference to `Deno` would not resolve there. It is also run by
  25:  *      Deno inside the edge runtime, where a node-only import would not resolve either. The
  26:  *      intersection of the two is plain TypeScript over plain data, which is all a decision needs.
  27:  *   2. NO I/O, NO CLOCK, NO RANDOMNESS. Every function here is pure. Storage, time and identity are
  28:  *      the caller's, so the same judgement can be replayed by a test without a database.
  29:  *
  30:  * WHICH PROGRAM ACTUALLY TYPE-CHECKS IT: the `tests/at` one, and ONLY that one. `bun run typecheck`
  31:  * runs `tsc -p` over the root project too, but that project's `include` is
  32:  * `["src/**\/*.ts", "src/**\/*.tsx", "vite.config.ts", "eslint.config.js"]`, so nothing under
  33:  * `supabase/` is in its program. This module reaches the strict acceptance program by being imported
  34:  * from `tests/at/suites/req-001/_fixture.ts`, and that import is the whole of its type coverage.
  35:  */
  36: 
  37: import { ACKNOWLEDGMENT_IDENTITY_COPY } from './acknowledgment-copy.ts';
  38: 
  39: /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ the two closed vocabularies */
  40: 
  41: /**
  42:  * The account types the PUBLIC signup surface offers — AT-001.07's second clause, as a value rather
  43:  * than as a sentence in a document. `platform_admin` is deliberately absent: it is provisioned, not
  44:  * signed up for, and `parseAccountType` below refuses it by construction rather than by a check
  45:  * somebody has to remember to write.
  46:  */
  47: export const PUBLIC_SIGNUP_ACCOUNT_TYPES = ['ngo', 'volunteer'] as const;
  48: 
  49: export type PublicSignupAccountType = (typeof PUBLIC_SIGNUP_ACCOUNT_TYPES)[number];
  50: 
  51: /**
  52:  * Every global type an account can hold, including the one no public path can produce. This mirrors
  53:  * the `public.account_type` enum in the migration; the two are separate statements of one fact, and
  54:  * the database is the one that wins — which is exactly why `public.complete_signup` re-checks the
  55:  * type itself instead of trusting this module (see `ngoOnlyActionAllowed`'s note on defence in
  56:  * depth, and the migration's own comment).
  57:  */
  58: export const ACCOUNT_TYPES = ['ngo', 'volunteer', 'platform_admin'] as const;
  59: 
  60: export type AccountType = (typeof ACCOUNT_TYPES)[number];
  61: 
  62: /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- the answer shape */
  63: 
  64: /**
  65:  * A decision, and its refusal carries WHY.
  66:  *
  67:  * A bare boolean would make every refusal indistinguishable at the call site, and AT-001.04 and
  68:  * AT-001.06 both assert that a refusal STATES its reason. Returning the reason rather than throwing
  69:  * it is deliberate too: these are expected outcomes of well-formed requests, not faults, and an
  70:  * exception would invite a caller to treat "a volunteer may not do this" the same way it treats a
  71:  * dropped database connection.
  72:  */
  73: export type Decision<T> = { ok: true; value: T } | { ok: false; reason: string };
  74: 
  75: function refuse<T>(reason: string): Decision<T> {
  76:   return { ok: false, reason };
  77: }
  78: 
  79: function accept<T>(value: T): Decision<T> {
  80:   return { ok: true, value };
  81: }
  82: 
  83: /* -------------------------------------------------------------- 1. what type is being asked for */
  84: 
  85: /**
  86:  * The requested account type, or a refusal naming why — and `platform_admin` can never come out of
  87:  * it, however it is spelled in the request.
  88:  *
  89:  * This is the only thing between an anonymous HTTP request and a minted platform administrator on
  90:  * the TypeScript side of the write path, which is why the database refuses the same value again
  91:  * (see the migration's `complete_signup`). One of the two being deleted or regressed must not be
  92:  * enough.
  93:  */
  94: export function parseAccountType(raw: unknown): Decision<PublicSignupAccountType> {
  95:   if (typeof raw !== 'string' || raw.trim() === '') {
  96:     return refuse(
  97:       `account type is required and must be one of ${PUBLIC_SIGNUP_ACCOUNT_TYPES.join(', ')}`,
  98:     );
  99:   }
 100:   const candidate = raw.trim();
 101:   if ((PUBLIC_SIGNUP_ACCOUNT_TYPES as readonly string[]).includes(candidate)) {
 102:     return accept(candidate as PublicSignupAccountType);
 103:   }
 104:   // Named separately from an outright unknown string, because the two are different mistakes and a
 105:   // caller that sees "unknown account type platform_admin" learns the wrong thing: the type is not
 106:   // unknown, it is not available here.
 107:   if (candidate === 'platform_admin') {
 108:     return refuse(
 109:       'platform_admin is not available through public signup — a platform administrator is provisioned, never self-signed-up',
 110:     );
 111:   }
 112:   return refuse(
 113:     `unknown account type ${JSON.stringify(candidate)} — public signup offers ${PUBLIC_SIGNUP_ACCOUNT_TYPES.join(', ')}`,
 114:   );
 115: }
 116: 
 117: /* ------------------------------------------------------ 2. is this completion request well-formed */
 118: 
 119: /** What arrives on the wire — every field `unknown`, because it came from a client. */
 120: export type CompleteSignupRequest = {
 121:   accountType?: unknown;
 122:   organizationName?: unknown;
 123:   acknowledgmentTextVersion?: unknown;
 124:   /** who is making the acknowledgment — AT-001.19's "name" */
 125:   signerName?: unknown;
 126:   /** the title they hold — AT-001.19's "title" */
 127:   signerTitle?: unknown;
 128:   /** the authority statement they affirm — AT-001.19's "authority attestation" */
 129:   authorityAttestation?: unknown;
 130: };
 131: 
 132: /**
 133:  * WHAT IS TRUE OF THE CALLER, as opposed to what the caller SAID — and the distinction is the whole
 134:  * security property of the volunteer gate below.
 135:  *
 136:  * `githubHandle` is the handle on an identity Supabase Auth has linked to this authenticated user.
 137:  * It arrives from `/auth/v1/user` by way of `extractGithubHandle` in `./github.ts`; it is NEVER a
 138:  * field of the request body, because a request field is something a client asserts about itself and
 139:  * a gate built on one gates nothing.
 140:  */
 141: export type CompleteSignupCaller = {
 142:   /** the linked GitHub identity's handle, or `null` when no GitHub identity is linked */
 143:   githubHandle: string | null;
 144: };
 145: 
 146: /** The same request once it has been judged: narrow, and safe to hand to the database. */
 147: export type ValidCompleteSignup = {
 148:   accountType: PublicSignupAccountType;
 149:   /** the NGO's organisation name; `null` for a volunteer, who has no organisation */
 150:   organizationName: string | null;
 151:   /** which version of the ToS + Platform Promise text was shown — AT-001.01 records it */
 152:   acknowledgmentTextVersion: string;
 153:   /**
 154:    * The linked GitHub handle this completion is judged against: non-null for a volunteer (the gate
 155:    * below refuses the completion otherwise), null for an NGO.
 156:    *
 157:    * It is CARRIED OUT rather than re-derived by the caller, so the value the write path uses is the
 158:    * same value the decision was made on. A caller that re-read the identity itself could write a
 159:    * profile under a handle the gate never saw.
 160:    */
 161:   githubHandle: string | null;
 162:   /**
 163:    * WHO SIGNED, AND UNDER WHAT AUTHORITY — AT-001.19's three fields, trimmed, carried out for the
 164:    * same reason `organizationName` is: the write path stores the values this decision judged.
 165:    *
 166:    * `authorityAttestation` is the STATEMENT that was affirmed, not a boolean. A `true` in a column
 167:    * records that something was clicked; the statement records WHAT was attested, exactly as
 168:    * `acknowledgmentTextVersion` records which text was accepted. The check below accepts one
 169:    * statement — `ACKNOWLEDGMENT_IDENTITY_COPY.authorityStatement` — so the stored value keeps
 170:    * today's rows distinguishable from those of any later statement.
 171:    */
 172:   signerName: string;
 173:   signerTitle: string;
 174:   authorityAttestation: string;
 175: };
 176: 
 177: /**
 178:  * Judge a completion request in full — the request, and the one FACT about the caller the volunteer
 179:  * branch turns on.
 180:  *
 181:  * THE VOLUNTEER BRANCH IS GATED ON A LINKED GITHUB IDENTITY. AT-001.04: "completion is blocked with
 182:  * the GitHub-link requirement stated; linking completes signup". The gate reads `caller.githubHandle`
 183:  * — a fact Supabase Auth reports about the authenticated user — and never a request field, so no
 184:  * client can assert its way past it. An NGO completion never reads the fact at all: AT-001.04's own
 185:  * scope is volunteer signup, and a gate that leaked onto NGOs would refuse a signup no criterion
 186:  * says anything about.
 187:  *
 188:  * IT FAILS CLOSED, AND THAT IS STATED BECAUSE THE EDGE ENTRY POINT HAS NO TYPE-CHECKER. Measured, not
 189:  * assumed: `bun run typecheck` covers the root project (`src/**`) and the `tests/at` program, and
 190:  * neither reaches an edge-function entry point (`supabase/functions/<name>/index.ts`). So a call
 191:  * site that forgets the second argument is
 192:  * a real possibility, and the reading below — `caller?.githubHandle`, absent or blank meaning "no
 193:  * linked identity" — makes that mistake BLOCK every volunteer completion loudly instead of waiving
 194:  * the gate silently. A gate that disappears when a caller is careless is not a gate.
 195:  *
 196:  * WHY THE JUDGED VALUE TRAVELS BACK OUT in `ValidCompleteSignup.githubHandle`: so the write path uses
 197:  * the handle this decision was made on rather than deriving its own, which is the same reason the
 198:  * organisation name comes back trimmed instead of being re-trimmed downstream.
 199:  *
 200:  * THE FOUR IDENTITY CHECKS COME LAST, AND THEIR PLACE IN THE ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING. AT-001.19 puts
 201:  * the acting person's name, title and authority attestation on every acknowledgment, and AT-001.39
 202:  * makes an omission a refusal. They are checked AFTER the acknowledgment text version, so every
 203:  * refusal an earlier criterion pins keeps firing first: `platform_admin` refuses at
 204:  * `parseAccountType`, an unlinked volunteer refuses at the GitHub gate, and a completion with no
 205:  * acknowledgment refuses at the text version. A request that omits the identity fields for one of
 206:  * those reasons never reaches these checks and its stated reason is unchanged.
 207:  *
 208:  * ONE REFUSAL PER CONDITION, EACH NAMING ITS OWN FIELD, because a caller told only that "something
 209:  * is missing" has nothing to correct. The fourth check is the one that makes the attestation mean
 210:  * anything: a non-blank string is not an attestation of authority — `'I am not authorized'` is a
 211:  * non-blank string — so the trimmed value must equal the SHIPPED statement in
 212:  * `./acknowledgment-copy.ts`, and anything else is refused as not matching it.
 213:  */
 214: export function validateCompleteSignup(
 215:   request: CompleteSignupRequest,
 216:   caller: CompleteSignupCaller,
 217: ): Decision<ValidCompleteSignup> {
 218:   const parsedType = parseAccountType(request.accountType);
 219:   if (!parsedType.ok) return refuse(parsedType.reason);
 220:   const accountType = parsedType.value;
 221: 
 222:   const rawName = request.organizationName;
 223:   let organizationName: string | null = null;
 224: 
 225:   if (accountType === 'ngo') {
 226:     if (typeof rawName !== 'string' || rawName.trim() === '') {
 227:       return refuse('an NGO signup must carry a non-empty organisation name');
 228:     }
 229:     organizationName = rawName.trim();
 230:   } else if (rawName !== undefined && rawName !== null) {
 231:     // Refused rather than ignored. A volunteer has no organisation, so a name arriving on a
 232:     // volunteer request means the caller and the server disagree about what is being created, and
 233:     // silently dropping it would let that disagreement reach the database as a missing row instead
 234:     // of surfacing as a refusal.
 235:     return refuse('a volunteer signup carries no organisation name — one account holds exactly one global type');
 236:   }
 237: 
 238:   // THE CALLER FACT, read defensively — see the fail-closed paragraph above. A missing argument, a
 239:   // null, or a blank string all mean the same thing here: no GitHub identity is linked.
 240:   const linkedGithubHandle =
 241:     typeof caller?.githubHandle === 'string' && caller.githubHandle.trim() !== '' ? caller.githubHandle.trim() : null;
 242: 
 243:   if (accountType === 'volunteer' && linkedGithubHandle === null) {
 244:     return refuse(
 245:       'a volunteer signup cannot be completed without a linked GitHub account — link GitHub to this account, then complete signup',
 246:     );
 247:   }
 248: 
 249:   const rawVersion = request.acknowledgmentTextVersion;
 250:   if (typeof rawVersion !== 'string' || rawVersion.trim() === '') {
 251:     return refuse(
 252:       'the ToS + Platform Promise acknowledgment text version is required — an acknowledgment that does not say which text was accepted records nothing',
 253:     );
 254:   }
 255: 
 256:   // (1) WHO SIGNED. A name is the field that makes the record about a person at all, and an
 257:   // acknowledgment that names nobody records nobody.
 258:   const rawSignerName = request.signerName;
 259:   if (typeof rawSignerName !== 'string' || rawSignerName.trim() === '') {
 260:     return refuse(
 261:       'the signer name is required — an acknowledgment records the person who made it, and one with no name records nobody',
 262:     );
 263:   }
 264: 
 265:   // (2) IN WHAT CAPACITY. The title is what makes the attestation of authority meaningful: a person
 266:   // binds an organisation in a role, and the record has to say which one.
 267:   const rawSignerTitle = request.signerTitle;
 268:   if (typeof rawSignerTitle !== 'string' || rawSignerTitle.trim() === '') {
 269:     return refuse(
 270:       'the signer title is required — an acknowledgment records the title the person held when they made it',
 271:     );
 272:   }
 273: 
 274:   // (3) AND THAT SOMETHING WAS ATTESTED AT ALL.
 275:   const rawAttestation = request.authorityAttestation;
 276:   if (typeof rawAttestation !== 'string' || rawAttestation.trim() === '') {
 277:     return refuse(
 278:       'the authority attestation is required — an acknowledgment with none records that nobody claimed the authority to make it',
 279:     );
 280:   }
 281: 
 282:   // (4) AND THAT WHAT WAS ATTESTED IS THE STATEMENT THIS PLATFORM SHIPS. See the header: a non-blank
 283:   // string is not an attestation of authority. The comparison is exact and against the trimmed
 284:   // value, so leading or trailing space is forgiven and content is not.
 285:   const authorityAttestation = rawAttestation.trim();
 286:   if (authorityAttestation !== ACKNOWLEDGMENT_IDENTITY_COPY.authorityStatement) {
 287:     return refuse(
 288:       'the authority attestation does not match the shipped authority statement — an acknowledgment records the statement that was affirmed, and it must be affirmed as shipped, word for word',
 289:     );
 290:   }
 291: 
 292:   return accept({
 293:     accountType,
 294:     organizationName,
 295:     acknowledgmentTextVersion: rawVersion.trim(),
 296:     signerName: rawSignerName.trim(),
 297:     signerTitle: rawSignerTitle.trim(),
 298:     authorityAttestation,
 299:     // NULL FOR AN NGO EVEN WHEN ONE IS LINKED. An NGO's account may well carry a GitHub identity —
 300:     // nothing forbids it — but the GitHub link and the onboarding import it fires are volunteer
 301:     // signup's, so an NGO completion carries no handle onward and writes no volunteer profile.
 302:     githubHandle: accountType === 'volunteer' ? linkedGithubHandle : null,
 303:   });
 304: }
 305: 
 306: /* --------------------------------------------------------- 2b. is this organisation name usable */
 307: 
 308: /**
 309:  * An organisation's name, trimmed, or a refusal naming why — the SAME rule `validateCompleteSignup`
 310:  * applies to the name arriving on an NGO signup, stated once.
 311:  *
 312:  * WHY IT IS HERE AND NOT AT ITS TWO CALL SITES, because that is where it used to be. The rule was
 313:  * written twice — once in `create-organization/index.ts`, a file no type-checker covers, and once
 314:  * in `tests/at/suites/req-001/_fixture.ts` — so the acceptance suite graded the adapter's copy and
 315:  * said nothing about the shipped one. Two independent statements of one rule with nothing able to
 316:  * notice them diverging is the exact defect this module exists to delete; it does not get an
 317:  * exception for being three lines long.
 318:  */
 319: export function validateOrganizationName(raw: unknown): Decision<string> {
 320:   if (typeof raw !== 'string' || raw.trim() === '') {
 321:     return refuse('an organisation needs a non-empty name');
 322:   }
 323:   return accept(raw.trim());
 324: }
 325: 
 326: /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3. may this account do NGO work */
 327: 
 328: /**
 329:  * AT-001.06, as a decision: an NGO-only action is permitted to an `ngo` account and to nothing else.
 330:  *
 331:  * A `platform_admin` is refused here too, and that is a deliberate reading of the criterion rather
 332:  * than an accident of the implementation. AT-001.06's clause is "one account holds exactly one
 333:  * global type; the NGO path requires a separate account", and creating an organisation as an NGO is
 334:  * the NGO path. The administrator's cross-account reach is AT-001.40, a different deliverable's id,
 335:  * declared red here — so granting it now would be building an untested requirement.
 336:  */
 337: export function ngoOnlyActionAllowed(accountType: unknown): Decision<'ngo'> {
 338:   if (accountType === 'ngo') return accept('ngo');
 339:   if (typeof accountType !== 'string' || !(ACCOUNT_TYPES as readonly string[]).includes(accountType)) {
 340:     return refuse('this action is available to NGO accounts only, and the caller holds no recognised account type');
 341:   }
 342:   return refuse(
 343:     `this action is available to NGO accounts only — the caller's account is of type ${JSON.stringify(accountType)}, ` +
 344:       'and one account holds exactly one global type, so the NGO path requires a separate account',
 345:   );
 346: }
 347: 
 348: /* -------------------------------------------------------------------- 4. what signup itself writes */
 349: 
 350: /**
 351:  * The one acknowledgment kind this leaf records: the ToS plus the Platform Promise, accepted once at
 352:  * signup by whoever completed it.
 353:  *
 354:  * It is a PLATFORM-level acknowledgment, not an organisation-level one, which is why
 355:  * `public.acknowledgments` keys on the account and not on the organisation, and why
 356:  * `public.has_platform_acknowledgment(account_id)` reads it back by account.
 357:  */
 358: export const PLATFORM_ACKNOWLEDGMENT_KIND = 'platform_tos_and_promise';
   1: /**
   2:  * `complete-signup` — turn an authenticated auth user into a typed account.
   3:  *
   4:  * THE CALLER HAS ALREADY AUTHENTICATED before it reaches here, and how is not this function's
   5:  * business: an email and a password through `auth.signUp`, or a Google or GitHub consent round trip
   6:  * through the OAuth redirect. Neither is a database call, so the standing rule that UI never touches
   7:  * the database directly holds — and this is exactly why AT-001.01 (email), AT-001.03 (Google) and
   8:  * AT-001.02 (GitHub) are the SAME code path. The difference between them is upstream, in how the
   9:  * session was obtained.
  10:  *
  11:  * WHAT IS *NOT* UPSTREAM is whether a GitHub identity is LINKED to the caller. That is a fact Auth
  12:  * reports about the user, this function reads it through `resolveCaller`, and a volunteer completion
  13:  * turns on it — AT-001.04. Linking itself happens inside Supabase Auth and reaches no code here, so
  14:  * the completion request is the only server-observable moment at which the mandatory link is
  15:  * satisfied; that is why the onboarding import fires here and not at some link event nothing can
  16:  * observe.
  17:  *
  18:  * WHAT IT DOES: sets the global account type once, and for an NGO creates the organisation, the
  19:  * `admin` membership and the ToS + Platform Promise acknowledgment with the address the gateway
  20:  * chain reported — NOT a verified source address; see `callerIp` in `../_shared/edge.ts` for the
  21:  * trust boundary and the measurement behind it — and the version of the text that was accepted, and
  22:  * WHO MADE IT: the person's name, their title, and the authority statement they affirmed (AT-001.19).
  23:  * and for a volunteer writes the imported GitHub profile. All of it through ONE call to
  24:  * `public.complete_signup`, which is one round trip and therefore one transaction — every row or
  25:  * none.
  26:  *
  27:  * ONE JOB. A second operation is a second function, never a switch on an action name: see
  28:  * `create-organization` beside this one.
  29:  */
