 136: -- THE DROP IS LOAD-BEARING, not tidiness.
 137: --
 138: -- `create function` with a new signature would OVERLOAD `public.complete_signup` rather than replace
 139: -- it, and PostgREST refuses an rpc name it cannot resolve to one function — the deployed edge
 140: -- function would start failing on a name that had worked for months, with an error about ambiguity
 141: -- that says nothing about this migration. Dropping first and recreating is one statement pair in one
 142: -- migration, which is one transaction: there is no window in which no `complete_signup` exists.
 143: --
 144: -- WHAT THE DROP DOES *NOT* GIVE, and an earlier draft of this comment claimed it did.
 145: -- `supabase/functions/complete-signup/index.ts` is the sole caller of this function in the tree and
 146: -- it is updated in the same commit — but SOURCE CO-LOCATION IS NOT DEPLOYMENT ATOMICITY. The
 147: -- database plane and the edge-function plane deploy separately, so any real environment has a window
 148: -- in which one is at the new version and the other is not, in whichever order it rolls. Saying "the
 149: -- caller is updated in the same change, therefore nothing can break" would be reasoning about a git
 150: -- diff and calling it a statement about a deployment.
 151: --
 152: -- THE BRIDGE, which is why that window is survivable rather than merely acknowledged:
 153: --   * THE FOUR NEW PARAMETERS ARE `default null`. PostgREST fills defaults on a NAMED-argument call,
 154: --     so a call carrying ONLY the original five names still resolves against this function. There is
 155: --     still exactly ONE `complete_signup` in the catalog, so the drop-first reasoning above is
 156: --     untouched — a default is not an overload.
 157: --   * THE NEW EDGE FUNCTION OMITS the four github keys from the rpc body when the judged handle is
