 413: /* ============================================================================ privilege posture == */
 414: 
 415: -- IDENTICAL TO THE FIRST MIGRATION'S, AND RE-STATED BECAUSE THE DROP TOOK THE OLD ONE WITH IT.
 416: --
 417: -- PostgreSQL grants EXECUTE on a new function to PUBLIC by default and every Data API role inherits
 418: -- that, so the freshly created function above is executable by `anon` until this revoke runs. The
 419: -- first migration found that by measuring with `has_function_privilege` rather than by reasoning,
 420: -- and the same measurement is repeated against this signature in the migration-replay capture.
 421: revoke execute on function public.complete_signup(uuid, text, text, text, inet, text, text[], integer, text) from public;
 422: grant execute on function public.complete_signup(uuid, text, text, text, inet, text, text[], integer, text) to service_role;
 423: 
 424: -- NO GRANT STATEMENT IS NOT THE SAME THING AS NO PRIVILEGE, and this comment used to confuse the
 425: -- two. It said "NO GRANT ON `public.volunteer_profiles` TO ANY ROLE" and inferred from the absence
 426: -- of a grant statement that the three Data API roles held nothing. THE CATALOG SAID OTHERWISE: the
 427: -- committed replay capture for this migration recorded REFERENCES, TRIGGER and TRUNCATE for `anon`,
 428: -- `authenticated` AND `service_role` on this table — Supabase ships ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES for
 429: -- `public`, so a new table there arrives already granted, exactly as a new function arrives
 430: -- executable by PUBLIC. The first migration measured that fact for function EXECUTE with
 431: -- `has_function_privilege` instead of reasoning about it; this is the same measurement applied to a
 432: -- table, and it changed the answer.
 433: --
 434: -- SO THE REVOKE IS THE THING THAT MAKES THE POSTURE TRUE. What was standing before it is small but
 435: -- real: none of the three privileges is reachable through PostgREST's verb surface,
 436: -- `[api] auto_expose_new_tables` is unset, and `service_role` is not a login role — but TRUNCATE is
 437: -- NOT subject to row-level security, so the privilege existed even though no route to it did, and a
 438: -- migration must not claim a posture the catalog contradicts.
 439: --
 440: -- The `postgres` owner rows remain after this and are not a defect: an owner's implicit privileges
 441: -- are not grants, and revoking them from the schema owner would break the definer function that is
 442: -- the only writer here. The re-run replay capture must show ZERO rows for the three roles below.
 443: revoke all on table public.volunteer_profiles from anon, authenticated, service_role;
 444: 
 445: -- WHAT REMAINS TRUE from the sentence this replaces: the table is unreachable through the Data API
 446: -- entirely, which is stricter than the grant-and-deny-by-policy posture `public.accounts` needs, and
 447: -- appropriate here because nothing client-side reads it yet. The service role writes it only through
 448: -- the definer function above.
 449: 
 450: -- PostgREST caches the schema. Without this, the first call to the recreated function is a 404 from
 451: -- the schema cache rather than a real answer — and after a DROP the stale entry would name a
 452: -- signature that no longer exists.
 453: notify pgrst, 'reload schema';

