   2:  * THE IDS REQ-001 HAS NOT LANDED YET, and the leaf that will land each one. There are 21 of them as
   3:  * of the acknowledgment-identity leaf; there were 24 before it, 28 before that, 30 before that and
   4:  * 33 before that, and the count moves down as leaves land.
   5:  *
   6:  * WHY THEY EXIST AT ALL. `harness/check.ts`'s `bijectionProblems()` refuses a run when any expected
   7:  * id has no registered call site, and `runner.ts` turns any such problem into exit 2 with NOTHING
   8:  * graded. The expected set is every `AT-001.NN (P0)` in `.taskmaster/docs/acceptance/at-req-001.md`
   9:  * — all 37 of them. So the moment `tests/at/suites/req-001/` exists, all 37 need executable call
  10:  * sites; there is no partial suite. Sixteen are written — AT-001.01 through .07 across the first
  11:  * accounts leaf and the GitHub one, AT-001.09 and .10 with the verification leaf, AT-001.38, .12,
  12:  * .13 and .14 with the session-and-reset one, and AT-001.19, .39 and .20 with the
  13:  * acknowledgment-identity one. The other 21 are declared, not faked: each one throws, loudly,
  14:  * stamped with its own id and with the manifest leaf that will make it real.
  15:  *
  16:  * THEY CANNOT SILENTLY GO GREEN. `AtPending` is a thrown error, so the id is RED, and
  17:  * `tests/at/expected/req-001.json` declares that red by SHAPE — `expected.ts` rebuilds the anchored
  18:  * prefix `AtPending: <id> PENDING [sut-missing] — ` from the declaration and compares it from
  19:  * position 0. A red of any other cause fails the declaration instead of hiding inside it, and a red
  20:  * that turns green fails it too, so a later leaf must update the ledger in the same change that
  21:  * lands its id. That file is REQ-001's live progress ledger from this item onward.
  22:  *
  23:  * WHAT THE PREFIX DOES NOT CHECK, said plainly because it is the reason every leaf writes a ledger
  24:  * of its own (`loop/items/AI4DEV-57/pending-ledger.txt`, then
  25:  * `loop/items/AI4DEV-58/pending-ledger.txt`, then `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/pending-ledger.txt`, then
  26:  * `loop/items/AI4DEV-60/pending-ledger.txt`, then `loop/items/AI4DEV-65/pending-ledger.txt`): the
  27:  * tail after the em dash is FREE. `expected.ts`
  28:  * anchors on the prefix only, so a stub whose detail read "todo" would pass every command in this
  29:  * repository. Nothing mechanical holds the detail below to the truth. The check that does is a
  30:  * written one — every leaf named here appears in `loop/decomp/req-001.md`, and the ledger is the
  31:  * artifact a reviewer compares against the manifest.
  32:  */
  33: 
  34: import { AtPending, type AtContext } from './_bind.ts';
  35: 
  36: /**
  37:  * The manifest's leaves, in the manifest's own words shortened to a recall hint. The keys are the
  38:  * deliverable-and-leaf labels from `loop/decomp/req-001.md`; the letters there are the MANIFEST's
  39:  * deliverables and are not the plan's decision letters, which use the same alphabet for a different
  40:  * purpose.
  41:  */
  42: export const LEAF = {
  43:   // FOUR LABELS ARE GONE FROM THIS MAP rather than kept for symmetry, and the rule is the same for
  44:   // all four: a leaf label sitting here with nothing pointing at it is a claim that something is
  45:   // still pending when nothing is. `tests/at/expected/req-001.json` declares the landed ids green
  46:   // in the same change each time.
  47:   //   D1.L2 — GitHub OAuth signup and the mandatory GitHub link — landed AT-001.02, .04 and .05.
  48:   //   D2.L1 — email verification and the unverified-write gate on Discovery messages — landed
  49:   //           AT-001.09 and .10, its only two ids.
  50:   //   D2.L2 — session expiry and revocation, auto-refresh, password reset, wrong-password
  51:   //           rejection — landed AT-001.38, .12, .13 and .14, its only four ids.
  52:   //   D4.L1 — acknowledgment identity capture, name, title and authority attestation — landed
  53:   //           AT-001.19, .39 and .20, its only three ids, so it is removed here by this leaf.
  54:   D3_L1: 'D3.L1 (per-NGO admin/member roles and multi-NGO membership isolation)',
  55:   D3_L2: 'D3.L2 (the single-seat NGO and the single-dev project invariant)',
  56:   D3_L3: 'D3.L3 (the cross-surface single-seat integration)',
  57:   D5_L1: 'D5.L1 (cross-NGO denial and unassigned-volunteer denial)',
  58:   D5_L2: 'D5.L2 (assigned-volunteer scope, platform-admin reach, logged-out visibility)',
  59:   D6_L1: 'D6.L1 (contact transfer, lost-access recovery and the escalation contact)',
  60:   D6_L2: 'D6.L2 (the lifecycle gate every write route registers through)',
  61:   D6_L3: 'D6.L3 (the append-only audit for role changes and transfer, and sign-in rate limiting)',
  62: } as const;
  63: 
  64: export type LeafLabel = (typeof LEAF)[keyof typeof LEAF];
  65: 
  66: /**
  67:  * One not-yet-landed id's whole body.
  68:  *
  69:  * `sut-missing` rather than `harness-missing` is the honest phase word: the harness is up — this
  70:  * suite's written ids run against it — and what is absent is the system under test for these
  71:  * criteria. The detail is what makes that word honest, by naming WHICH system under test.
  72:  *
  73:  * (The sentence above used to say "this suite's own four ids", which was true when four were
  74:  * written and false from the next leaf onward. The count lives in this file's header and in the
  75:  * ledger, and stating it a second time here only created somewhere for it to drift.)
  76:  */
  77: export function notLanded(leaf: LeafLabel): (ctx: AtContext) => Promise<void> {
  78:   return async (ctx: AtContext): Promise<void> => {
  79:     throw new AtPending(ctx.atId, 'sut-missing', `REQ-001 ${leaf} has not landed`);
  80:   };
  81: }
