 can tell REACHABILITY from a 404; it cannot tell WHICH directory the edge runtime\n127: # mounted. So the launch is written down instead of inferred.\n128: #\n129: # COMMAND: bunx supabase functions serve\n130: # WORKING DIRECTORY: C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\n131: # FLAGS: none. --no-verify-jwt is NOT passed, so the runtime verifies the caller's token exactly as\n132: #        it would in a deployment.\n133: # LAUNCHED: 2026-08-09 18:31:36 +03:00\n134: # PROCESS ID: 49328\n135: #\n136: # Its first lines of output, which name the two functions it mounted:\n137: 2026-08-09T15:30:55.680974747Z Serving functions on http://127.0.0.1:54321/functions/v1/<function-name>\n138: 2026-08-09T15:30:55.681020066Z  - http://127.0.0.1:54321/functions/v1/complete-signup\n139: 2026-08-09T15:30:55.681022631Z  - http://127.0.0.1:54321/functions/v1/create-organization\n140: 2026-08-09T15:30:55.681024475Z Using supabase-edge-runtime-1.74.2 (compatible with Deno v2.1.4)\n141: #\n142: # And the probe the proof script performs, run here by hand as well: an unauthenticated POST to\n143: # complete-signup answers HTTP 401 - a refusal, not a 404, so the function is being served.\n144: #\n145: # WHY THE MOUNT'S REVISION IS NOT LOAD-BEARING, verified in the tree before this run:\n146: #   git diff main...HEAD --stat -- supabase/functions/\n147: #     supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts | 167 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++\n148: #     1 file changed, 167 insertions(+)\n149: #   complete-signup/index.ts imports only _shared/accounts.ts, _shared/github.ts and _shared/edge.ts.\n150: # This branch therefore changes nothing complete-signup imports, and every revision it could be\n151: # compared against is byte for byte the same code.\n152: #\n153: # STOPPED AFTER STEP 5 FINISHED, on purpose. A `functions serve` left alive against a worktree that\n154: # is later removed is the exact stale-mount hazard this file's own comment describes, and this item\n155: # has no further use for it: nothing else in the item calls an edge function, and continuous\n156: # integration has no database. The edge runtime CONTAINER that `supabase start` runs stays up and\n157: # still answers 401, which is the stack's normal state and not this launch.\n158: ----- the serve process was stopped by the executor after step 5 -----\n\n(End of file - total 158 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# AI4DEV-59 (email verification, unverified-write gate) - STEP 4: THE STACK WITH THE FLIPPED CONFIG\n# Started: 2026-08-09 18:27:51 +03:00\n# Worktree: C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\n# Branch:   nirdrang/ai4dev-59-email-verification-and-the-gate-on-unverified-writing-d2l1\n#\n# LOCAL DEVELOPMENT KEYS ARE REDACTED BELOW, exactly as the predecessor item's stack-up\n# transcript redacts them. They are local-stack values reachable only at 127.0.0.1 on the\n# machine that ran this, and nothing key-shaped is committed. GitHub push protection once\n# REFUSED a push in this project for exactly this file's predecessor, so the rule has a\n# mechanical enforcer. What is redacted: the publishable key, the secret key, the local JWT\n# signing secret, both demo JWTs (anon and service role) and the two S3 protocol credentials.\n# What is NOT redacted, because none of it is a credential: every URL and port, and the\n# postgres/postgres local database URL the Supabase CLI prints on every machine.\n#\n# WHY THE STACK IS STOPPED AND STARTED RATHER THAN ONLY RESET. Auth reads config.toml when its\n# container is created. The stack that was already running was created BEFORE this branch flipped\n# [auth.email] enable_confirmations to true, and its container proved it - the reading is the\n# first command below. A db reset alone would have left that container in place, and every\n# check in step 5 would have measured the OLD setting.\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\stack-up.txt","text":"# AI4DEV-59 (email verification, unverified-write gate) - STEP 4: THE STACK WITH THE FLIPPED CONFIG\n# Started: 2026-08-09 18:27:51 +03:00\n# Worktree: C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\n# Branch:   nirdrang/ai4dev-59-email-verification-and-the-gate-on-unverified-writing-d2l1\n#\n# LOCAL DEVELOPMENT KEYS ARE REDACTED BELOW, exactly as the predecessor item's stack-up\n# transcript redacts them. They are local-stack values reachable only at 127.0.0.1 on the\n# machine that ran this, and nothing key-shaped is committed. GitHub push protection once\n# REFUSED a push in this project for exactly this file's predecessor, so the rule has a\n# mechanical enforcer. What is redacted: the publishable key, the secret key, the local JWT\n# signing secret, both demo JWTs (anon and service role) and the two S3 protocol credentials.\n# What is NOT redacted, because none of it is a credential: every URL and port, and the\n# postgres/postgres local database URL the Supabase CLI prints on every machine.\n#\n# WHY THE STACK IS STOPPED AND STARTED RATHER THAN ONLY RESET. Auth reads config.toml when its\n# container is created. The stack that was already running was created BEFORE this branch flipped\n# [auth.email] enable_confirmations to true, and its container proved it - the reading is the\n# first command below. A db reset alone would have left that container in place, and every\n# check in step 5 would have measured the OLD setting.\n\n===== BEFORE: the running auth container's confirmation setting - GOTRUE_MAILER_AUTOCONFIRM=true means confirmations are OFF =====\n$ docker inspect supabase_auth_poancmeitlmxejofwzuu --format '{{range .Config.Env}}{{println .}}{{end}}' | Select-String -Pattern \"AUTOCONFIRM|RATE_LIMIT_EMAIL\"\n\nGOTRUE_MAILER_AUTOCONFIRM=true\nGOTRUE_SMS_AUTOCONFIRM=true\nGOTRUE_RATE_LIMIT_EMAIL_SENT=360000\n----- exit code: 0 -----\n\n===== bun run db:stop =====\n$ bun run db:stop\nbun : $ bunx supabase stop\nAt line:1 char:1\n+ bun run db:stop\n+ ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n    + CategoryInfo          : NotSpecified: ($ bunx supabase stop:String) [], RemoteException\n    + FullyQualifiedErrorId : NativeCommandError\n \n{\"project_id_filter\":\"poancmeitlmxejofwzuu\",\"backup\":true,\"message\":\"Stopped supabase local development setup.\"}\n----- exit code: 0 -----\n\n===== bun run db:start - the stack comes back up, and its auth container is created from THIS branch's config.toml =====\n$ bun run db:start\nbun : $ bunx supabase start\nAt line:1 char:1\n+ bun run db:start\n+ ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n    + CategoryInfo          : NotSpecified: ($ bunx supabase start:String) [], RemoteException\n    + FullyQualifiedErrorId : NativeCommandError\n \n{\"DB_URL\":\"postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:54322/postgres\",\"API_URL\":\"http://127.0.0.1:54321\",\"REST_URL\":\"http://127.0.0.1:54321/rest/v1\",\"GRAPHQL_URL\":\"http://127.0.0.1:54321/graphql/v1\",\"FUNCTIONS_URL\":\"http://127.0.0.1:54321/functions/v1\",\"MCP_URL\":\"http://127.0.0.1:54321/mcp\",\"STUDIO_URL\":\"http://127.0.0.1:54323\",\"PUBLISHABLE_KEY\":\"<REDACTED-local-publishable-key>\",\"SECRET_KEY\":\"<REDACTED-local-secret-key>\",\"JWT_SECRET\":\"<REDACTED-local-jwt-secret>\",\"ANON_KEY\":\"<REDACTED-local-demo-jwt>\",\"SERVICE_ROLE_KEY\":\"<REDACTED-local-demo-jwt>\",\"MAILPIT_URL\":\"http://127.0.0.1:54324\",\"INBUCKET_URL\":\"http://127.0.0.1:54324\",\"STORAGE_S3_URL\":\"http://127.0.0.1:54321/storage/v1/s3\",\"S3_PROTOCOL_ACCESS_KEY_ID\":\"<REDACTED>\",\"S3_PROTOCOL_ACCESS_KEY_SECRET\":\"<REDACTED>\",\"S3_PROTOCOL_REGION\":\"local\",\"message\":\"\"}\n----- exit code: 0 -----\n\n===== AFTER: the NEW auth container's confirmation setting - GOTRUE_MAILER_AUTOCONFIRM=false means confirmations are ON =====\n$ docker inspect supabase_auth_poancmeitlmxejofwzuu --format '{{range .Config.Env}}{{println .}}{{end}}' | Select-String -Pattern \"AUTOCONFIRM|RATE_LIMIT_EMAIL\"\n\nGOTRUE_RATE_LIMIT_EMAIL_SENT=360000\nGOTRUE_SMS_AUTOCONFIRM=true\nGOTRUE_MAILER_AUTOCONFIRM=false\n----- exit code: 0 -----\n\n# MEASURED, AND NOT WHAT THE FILE SAYS: the auth container carries\n# GOTRUE_RATE_LIMIT_EMAIL_SENT=360000 while supabase/config.toml carries [auth.rate_limit]\n# email_sent = 2. The CLI did not push the file's value into the container. So the checked-in\n# number is NOT the cap this run measures against. Step 5 check (f) reports the file's value and\n# whether the mailer actually refused anything; this line records why those two can disagree.\n\n===== bun run db:reset =====\n$ bun run db:reset\nbun : $ bunx supabase db reset\nAt line:1 char:1\n+ bun run db:reset\n+ ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n    + CategoryInfo          : NotSpecified: ($ bunx supabase db reset:String) [], RemoteException\n    + FullyQualifiedErrorId : NativeCommandError\n \nResetting local database...\nWARN: environment variable is unset: SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID\nWARN: environment variable is unset: SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GOOGLE_SECRET\nWARN: environment variable is unset: SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID\nWARN: environment variable is unset: SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GITHUB_SECRET\nRecreating database...\nInitialising schema...\nSeeding globals from roles.sql...\nSkipping migration .gitkeep... (file name must match pattern \"<timestamp>_name.sql\")\nSkipping migration README.md... (file name must match pattern \"<timestamp>_name.sql\")\nApplying migration 20260808120000_accounts_org_membership_and_acknowledgments.sql...\nApplying migration 20260809090000_volunteer_github_link_and_imported_profile.sql...\nWARN: no files matched pattern: supabase/seed.sql\nSkipping migration .gitkeep... (file name must match pattern \"<timestamp>_name.sql\")\nSkipping migration README.md... (file name must match pattern \"<timestamp>_name.sql\")\nRestarting containers...\nA new version of Supabase CLI is available: v2.113.0 (currently installed v2.110.0)\nWe recommend updating regularly for new features and bug fixes: https://supabase.com/docs/guides/cli/getting-started#updating-the-supabase-cli\nWARN: environment variable is unset: SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID\nWARN: environment variable is unset: SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GOOGLE_SECRET\nWARN: environment variable is unset: SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID\nWARN: environment variable is unset: SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GITHUB_SECRET\nA new version of Supabase CLI is available: v2.113.0 (currently installed v2.110.0)\nWe recommend updating regularly for new features and bug fixes: https://supabase.com/docs/guides/cli/getting-started#updating-the-supabase-cli\nFinished \u001b[36msupabase db reset\u001b[39m on branch \u001b[36mmain\u001b[39m.\n{\"target\":\"local\",\"version\":\"\",\"message\":\"Reset local database.\"}\n----- exit code: 0 -----\n\n===== bunx supabase status - the stack is up after the reset =====\n$ bunx supabase status\nbunx : Stopped services: [supabase_imgproxy_poancmeitlmxejofwzuu supabase_pooler_poancmeitlmxejofwzuu]\nAt line:1 char:1\n+ bunx supabase status\n+ ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n    + CategoryInfo          : NotSpecified: (Stopped service...eitlmxejofwzuu]:String) [], RemoteException\n    + FullyQualifiedErrorId : NativeCommandError\n \n{\"DB_URL\":\"postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:54322/postgres\",\"API_URL\":\"http://127.0.0.1:54321\",\"REST_URL\":\"http://127.0.0.1:54321/rest/v1\",\"GRAPHQL_URL\":\"http://127.0.0.1:54321/graphql/v1\",\"FUNCTIONS_URL\":\"http://127.0.0.1:54321/functions/v1\",\"MCP_URL\":\"http://127.0.0.1:54321/mcp\",\"STUDIO_URL\":\"http://127.0.0.1:54323\",\"PUBLISHABLE_KEY\":\"<REDACTED-local-publishable-key>\",\"SECRET_KEY\":\"<REDACTED-local-secret-key>\",\"JWT_SECRET\":\"<REDACTED-local-jwt-secret>\",\"ANON_KEY\":\"<REDACTED-local-demo-jwt>\",\"SERVICE_ROLE_KEY\":\"<REDACTED-local-demo-jwt>\",\"MAILPIT_URL\":\"http://127.0.0.1:54324\",\"INBUCKET_URL\":\"http://127.0.0.1:54324\",\"STORAGE_S3_URL\":\"http://127.0.0.1:54321/storage/v1/s3\",\"S3_PROTOCOL_ACCESS_KEY_ID\":\"<REDACTED>\",\"S3_PROTOCOL_ACCESS_KEY_SECRET\":\"<REDACTED>\",\"S3_PROTOCOL_REGION\":\"local\",\"message\":\"\"}\n----- exit code: 0 -----\n\n===== the auth container AFTER the reset, re-read - the flip survives the container restart =====\n$ docker inspect supabase_auth_poancmeitlmxejofwzuu --format '{{range .Config.Env}}{{println .}}{{end}}' | Select-String -Pattern \"AUTOCONFIRM|RATE_LIMIT_EMAIL\"\n\nGOTRUE_RATE_LIMIT_EMAIL_SENT=360000\nGOTRUE_SMS_AUTOCONFIRM=true\nGOTRUE_MAILER_AUTOCONFIRM=false\n----- exit code: 0 -----\n\n===== bunx supabase functions serve - THE OPERATOR-SIDE BINDING OF THE MOUNT TO THIS WORKTREE =====\n# Gate 2 ruling [A5] asks for this record, and this is it. The stale-mount probe inside\n# proof-local.ts can tell REACHABILITY from a 404; it cannot tell WHICH directory the edge runtime\n# mounted. So the launch is written down instead of inferred.\n#\n# COMMAND: bunx supabase functions serve\n# WORKING DIRECTORY: C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\n# FLAGS: none. --no-verify-jwt is NOT passed, so the runtime verifies the caller's token exactly as\n#        it would in a deployment.\n# LAUNCHED: 2026-08-09 18:31:36 +03:00\n# PROCESS ID: 49328\n#\n# Its first lines of output, which name the two functions it mounted:\n2026-08-09T15:30:55.680974747Z Serving functions on http://127.0.0.1:54321/functions/v1/<function-name>\n2026-08-09T15:30:55.681020066Z  - http://127.0.0.1:54321/functions/v1/complete-signup\n2026-08-09T15:30:55.681022631Z  - http://127.0.0.1:54321/functions/v1/create-organization\n2026-08-09T15:30:55.681024475Z Using supabase-edge-runtime-1.74.2 (compatible with Deno v2.1.4)\n#\n# And the probe the proof script performs, run here by hand as well: an unauthenticated POST to\n# complete-signup answers HTTP 401 - a refusal, not a 404, so the function is being served.\n#\n# WHY THE MOUNT'S REVISION IS NOT LOAD-BEARING, verified in the tree before this run:\n#   git diff main...HEAD --stat -- supabase/functions/\n#     supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts | 167 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++\n#     1 file changed, 167 insertions(+)\n#   complete-signup/index.ts imports only _shared/accounts.ts, _shared/github.ts and _shared/edge.ts.\n# This branch therefore changes nothing complete-signup imports, and every revision it could be\n# compared against is byte for byte the same code.\n#\n# STOPPED AFTER STEP 5 FINISHED, on purpose. A `functions serve` left alive against a worktree that\n# is later removed is the exact stale-mount hazard this file's own comment describes, and this item\n# has no further use for it: nothing else in the item calls an edge function, and continuous\n# integration has no database. The edge runtime CONTAINER that `supabase start` runs stays up and\n# still answers 401, which is the stack's normal state and not this launch.\n----- the serve process was stopped by the executor after step 5 -----","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":158,"totalLines":158,"truncated":false}},"title":"loop\\items\\AI4DEV-59\\stack-up.txt","time":{"start":1786290410278,"end":1786290410285}},"id":"prt_fe734920e001hrETZwHx3JONJu","sessionID":"ses_018cbdef8ffeIiDUqdR6dh2IlP","messageID":"msg_fe7347d31001c96tg63DqQs2yY"}}
0c389c633b9867b93b1466a77c75d5bc9df66f56:loop/items/AI4DEV-59/artifacts/audit-luna.stderr.log:10248:C:\Users\nirdr\Downloads\ai4good\.claude\worktrees\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\loop\items\AI4DEV-59\artifacts\audit-flash.events.jsonl:23:{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1786290419759,"sessionID":"ses_018cbdef8ffeIiDUqdR6dh2IlP","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01_iQwl272ANJrGcNAJHP1R0950","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\supabase\\functions\\_shared\\verification.ts"},"output":"<path>C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\supabase\\functions\\_shared\\verification.ts</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: /**\n2:  * THE TWO EMAIL-VERIFICATION JUDGEMENTS REQ-001's D2.L1 leaf ships, in ONE module.\n3:  *\n4:  * The PRD makes email verification \"the floor for any Discovery message\" and says vetting is\n5:  * \"never the Discovery wall\" (decision-8, `.taskmaster/docs/prd-new.md` line 787). This module is\n6:  * that floor, written as a decision rather than as a sentence in a document.\n7:  *\n8:  * ============================================================================================\n9:  * THE GATE HAS NO DEPLOYED CALLER YET. IT IS THE HOOK THE DISCOVERY ROUTE MUST CALL.\n10:  * ============================================================================================\n11:  *\n12:  * No Discovery surface exists anywhere in this repository — no table, no edge function, no\n13:  * screen. The Discovery message route belongs to REQ-002/004, and\n14:  * `.taskmaster/docs/migration-nextjs-to-tanstack-VERIFIED.md` maps it to a future\n15:  * `discovery-message` edge function. Building that route here would be another requirement's\n16:  * surface invented early, so this leaf ships the DECISION that route must consult and nothing\n17:  * else. It is the same pattern the suite already ratifies for `hasPlatformAcknowledgment`\n18:  * (`tests/at/suites/req-001/_contract.ts`): enforcement a leaf cannot build is named as the hook\n19:  * the later leaf calls.\n20:  *\n21:  * SO: NO GREEN FROM THIS ITEM MAY BE READ AS \"DISCOVERY MESSAGING IS GATED IN PRODUCTION.\"\n22:  * What a green says is that this module answers as the two criteria require, and that the\n23:  * acceptance suite's stand-in surface consults it. Whether the real route calls it is the real\n24:  * route's leaf to prove, and that leaf does not exist yet.\n25:  *\n26:  * THE GATE IS TYPE-BLIND, AND THAT IS A DECISION RATHER THAN AN OMISSION. Decision-8 makes\n27:  * verification the floor for ANY Discovery message. AT-001.10's unverified-NGO subject is an\n28:  * INSTANCE of that rule, not its scope. A gate that read the account type would ship a\n29:  * distinction no ratified text draws, so `discoveryMessageAllowed` never sees an account type.\n30:  *\n31:  * WHY A NEW MODULE RATHER THAN GROWING `accounts.ts`: that file's header scopes it to the\n32:  * account deliverable's decisions; `github.ts` already set the several-small-modules precedent;\n33:  * and this rule's future caller is the Discovery send route, not signup.\n34:  *\n35:  * TWO CONSTRAINTS THIS FILE IS UNDER, the same two `accounts.ts` and `github.ts` are under, and\n36:  * both are real rather than stylistic:\n37:  *\n38:  *   1. ZERO NON-RELATIVE IMPORTS AND NO DENO GLOBAL. It is compiled by `tests/at/tsconfig.json`\n39:  *      (`strict`, `skipLibCheck: false`, `types: [\"node\"]`, no DOM library) and it is also run by\n40:  *      Deno inside the edge runtime. A bare `jsr:`/`npm:` specifier or a `Deno` reference would\n41:  *      resolve in neither. The intersection of the two is plain TypeScript over plain data.\n42:  *   2. NO I/O, NO CLOCK, NO RANDOMNESS. Both functions below are pure, so the same judgement can\n43:  *      be replayed by a test with no network and no database.\n44:  *\n45:  * WHICH PROGRAM TYPE-CHECKS IT: the `tests/at` one, and only that one. It reaches that strict\n46:  * program by being imported from `tests/at/suites/req-001/_fixture.ts` and from\n47:  * `tests/at/harness/shipped-verification.selftest.ts`, exactly as `accounts.ts` does, and those\n48:  * imports are the whole of its type coverage.\n49:  *\n50:  * ============================================================================================\n51:  * BOTH FUNCTIONS FAIL CLOSED, AND THAT IS STATED BECAUSE THE EDGE ENTRY POINT HAS NO\n52:  * TYPE-CHECKER.\n53:  * ============================================================================================\n54:  *\n55:  * Measured, not assumed, and the measurement is `accounts.ts`'s: `bun run typecheck` covers the\n56:  * root project (`src/**`) and the `tests/at` program, and neither reaches an edge-function entry\n57:  * point (`supabase/functions/<name>/index.ts`). So a call site that forgets an argument, or that\n58:  * hands over a response body it never checked, is a real possibility rather than a hypothetical\n59:  * one. Every unrecognised shape below therefore reads as UNVERIFIED and every malformed caller is\n60:  * REFUSED. A gate that disappears when a caller is careless is not a gate.\n61:  *\n62:  * The fail-closed promise has a direct oracle rather than only this paragraph:\n63:  * `tests/at/harness/shipped-verification.selftest.ts` drives every malformed shape named here.\n64:  * A promise with no oracle is an untrue stated fact waiting to happen.\n65:  *\n66:  * WHAT THIS MODULE DOES NOT DO: it never sets `email_confirmed_at`, never sends an email, and\n67:  * never follows a link. Verified is Supabase Auth's own fact, written by GoTrue when the emailed\n68:  * link is used. This leaf ships no migration and `public.accounts` gains no column, so there is\n69:  * no second place where \"verified\" is recorded and no two records to disagree.\n70:  *\n71:  * AND IT ASSERTS NOTHING ABOUT LINK EXPIRY, SINGLE USE, OR RESEND. AT-001.11 is retired —\n72:  * \"verification-link expiry/single-use/resend semantics are not stated in REQ-001\"\n73:  * (`.taskmaster/docs/acceptance/at-req-001.md` line 22) — so no code here models those semantics.\n74:  */\n75: \n76: import type { Decision } from './accounts.ts';\n77: \n78: /* --------------------------------------------------- 1. is this caller's email address verified */\n79: \n80: /**\n81:  * GoTrue's field for the instant a user's email address was confirmed.\n82:  *\n83:  * Named as a constant rather than spelled inline for the reason `github.ts` gives about\n84:  * `identity_data.user_name`: it is a VENDOR's field name, so if it ever turns out to be spelled\n85:  * differently, the change is a one-place change and a reader can find every use of it by\n86:  * searching for one identifier.\n87:  */\n88: export const EMAIL_CONFIRMED_AT_FIELD = 'email_confirmed_at';\n89: \n90: /**\n91:  * Whether the caller's email address is verified — judged from the GoTrue `/auth/v1/user`\n92:  * response shape and from nothing else.\n93:  *\n94:  * IT TAKES `unknown` ON PURPOSE, exactly as `extractGithubHandle` does. The argument is a JSON\n95:  * body that crossed a network; typing the parameter as a tidy record would be a claim about\n96:  * somebody else's server. So every step down into it is checked here instead.\n97:  *\n98:  * THE ONLY VERIFIED ANSWER is a `email_confirmed_at` that is a string with something in it. A\n99:  * missing field, `null`, an empty or blank string, a number, a boolean, an object, an array, and\n100:  * a user that is not an object at all ALL read as unverified. That list is not defensive\n101:  * decoration — it is the fail-closed direction, and it is the safe one here: the gate below\n102:  * refuses on unverified, so a shape this does not recognise BLOCKS a write loudly instead of\n103:  * waiving the floor quietly.\n104:  *\n105:  * BLANK IS UNVERIFIED, and that is a deliberate reading rather than an accident of using `trim`.\n106:  * A timestamp of three spaces is not an instant, and treating it as one would let the single\n107:  * value most likely to arrive from a broken serialiser mean \"verified\".\n108:  *\n109:  * WHAT IS NOT PROVED BY ANY LOOP-TIER GREEN over this function: that the shape it reads is the\n110:  * shape GoTrue really sends. The fixture renders the canonical shape, which is a prediction of\n111:  * the vendor. The binding measurement is the live proof — `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts`\n112:  * check (d) feeds a REAL `/auth/v1/user` response of a confirmed user to this function.\n113:  */\n114: export function emailVerifiedFromUser(user: unknown): boolean {\n115:   if (typeof user !== 'object' || user === null) return false;\n116:   const confirmedAt = (user as Record<string, unknown>)[EMAIL_CONFIRMED_AT_FIELD];\n117:   return typeof confirmedAt === 'string' && confirmedAt.trim() !== '';\n118: }\n119: \n120: /* ------------------------------------------------- 2. may this caller send a Discovery message */\n121: \n122: /**\n123:  * WHAT IS TRUE OF THE CALLER, as opposed to what the caller SAID — the same distinction\n124:  * `CompleteSignupCaller` in `./accounts.ts` draws, and for the same security reason.\n125:  *\n126:  * `emailVerified` comes from `emailVerifiedFromUser` over the `/auth/v1/user` response for the\n127:  * authenticated user. It is NEVER a field of the request body: a request field is something a\n128:  * client asserts about itself, and a gate built on one gates nothing.\n129:  */\n130: export type DiscoveryMessageCaller = {\n131:   /** whether Supabase Auth reports this user's email address confirmed */\n132:   emailVerified: boolean;\n133: };\n134: \n135: /**\n136:  * AT-001.10, as a decision: a Discovery message is permitted to a caller whose email address is\n137:  * verified, and to nobody else.\n138:  *\n139:  * THE ONLY ALLOW IS `caller?.emailVerified === true`. Strict equality against `true`, not a\n140:  * truthiness test: a caller that passed the string `\"false\"`, or `1`, or any other value a\n141:  * careless JSON round trip produces, must not open the gate. This is the same fail-closed posture\n142:  * `validateCompleteSignup` takes on its caller fact, for the same measured reason — the edge\n143:  * entry point has no type-checker, so a missing or malformed argument is a real possibility.\n144:  *\n145:  * THE REFUSAL NAMES EMAIL VERIFICATION TWICE: as what is missing, and as the remedy. AT-001.10's\n146:  * words are \"the attempt is blocked with verification named as the remedy\", and a 403 saying\n147:  * \"forbidden\" would satisfy a weaker reading of that criterion while telling the sender nothing\n148:  * about what to do next. The test body matches the reason against `/verif/i` and `/email/i`.\n149:  *\n150:  * IT IS TYPE-BLIND — see the module header. No account type reaches this function, because\n151:  * decision-8's floor is under EVERY Discovery message and AT-001.10's NGO is one instance of it.\n152:  *\n153:  * IT SAYS NOTHING ABOUT VETTING. Decision-8 also says vetting is \"never the Discovery wall\", so a\n154:  * vetting state is not a second condition hiding in here — verification is the whole floor, and\n155:  * adding anything beside it would be building a wall the ratified text refuses.\n156:  */\n157: export function discoveryMessageAllowed(caller: DiscoveryMessageCaller): Decision<'verified'> {\n158:   if (caller?.emailVerified === true) {\n159:     return { ok: true, value: 'verified' };\n160:   }\n161:   return {\n162:     ok: false,\n163:     reason:\n164:       'a Discovery message needs a verified email address — this account is email-unverified. ' +\n165:       'Use the verification link sent to the account address, then send the message again',\n166:   };\n167: }\n\n(End of file - total 167 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"/**\n * THE TWO EMAIL-VERIFICATION JUDGEMENTS REQ-001's D2.L1 leaf ships, in ONE module.\n *\n * The PRD makes email verification \"the floor for any Discovery message\" and says vetting is\n * \"never the Discovery wall\" (decision-8, `.taskmaster/docs/prd-new.md` line 787). This module is\n * that floor, written as a decision rather than as a sentence in a document.\n *\n * ============================================================================================\n * THE GATE HAS NO DEPLOYED CALLER YET. IT IS THE HOOK THE DISCOVERY ROUTE MUST CALL.\n * ============================================================================================\n *\n * No Discovery surface exists anywhere in this repository — no table, no edge function, no\n * screen. The Discovery message route belongs to REQ-002/004, and\n * `.taskmaster/docs/migration-nextjs-to-tanstack-VERIFIED.md` maps it to a future\n * `discovery-message` edge function. Building that route here would be another requirement's\n * surface invented early, so this leaf ships the DECISION that route must consult and nothing\n * else. It is the same pattern the suite already ratifies for `hasPlatformAcknowledgment`\n * (`tests/at/suites/req-001/_contract.ts`): enforcement a leaf cannot build is named as the hook\n * the later leaf calls.\n *","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\supabase\\functions\\_shared\\verification.ts","text":"/**\n * THE TWO EMAIL-VERIFICATION JUDGEMENTS REQ-001's D2.L1 leaf ships, in ONE module.\n *\n * The PRD makes email verification \"the floor for any Discovery message\" and says vetting is\n * \"never the Discovery wall\" (decision-8, `.taskmaster/docs/prd-new.md` line 787). This module is\n * that floor, written as a decision rather than as a sentence in a document.\n *\n * ============================================================================================\n * THE GATE HAS NO DEPLOYED CALLER YET. IT IS THE HOOK THE DISCOVERY ROUTE MUST CALL.\n * ============================================================================================\n *\n * No Discovery surface exists anywhere in this repository — no table, no edge function, no\n * screen. The Discovery message route belongs to REQ-002/004, and\n * `.taskmaster/docs/migration-nextjs-to-tanstack-VERIFIED.md` maps it to a future\n * `discovery-message` edge function. Building that route here would be another requirement's\n * surface invented early, so this leaf ships the DECISION that route must consult and nothing\n * else. It is the same pattern the suite already ratifies for `hasPlatformAcknowledgment`\n * (`tests/at/suites/req-001/_contract.ts`): enforcement a leaf cannot build is named as the hook\n * the later leaf calls.\n *\n * SO: NO GREEN FROM THIS ITEM MAY BE READ AS \"DISCOVERY MESSAGING IS GATED IN PRODUCTION.\"\n * What a green says is that this module answers as the two criteria require, and that the\n * acceptance suite's stand-in surface consults it. Whether the real route calls it is the real\n * route's leaf to prove, and that leaf does not exist yet.\n *\n * THE GATE IS TYPE-BLIND, AND THAT IS A DECISION RATHER THAN AN OMISSION. Decision-8 makes\n * verification the floor for ANY Discovery message. AT-001.10's unverified-NGO subject is an\n * INSTANCE of that rule, not its scope. A gate that read the account type would ship a\n * distinction no ratified text draws, so `discoveryMessageAllowed` never sees an account type.\n *\n * WHY A NEW MODULE RATHER THAN GROWING `accounts.ts`: that file's header scopes it to the\n * account deliverable's decisions; `github.ts` already set the several-small-modules precedent;\n * and this rule's future caller is the Discovery send route, not signup.\n *\n * TWO CONSTRAINTS THIS FILE IS UNDER, the same two `accounts.ts` and `github.ts` are under, and\n * both are real rather than stylistic:\n *\n *   1. ZERO NON-RELATIVE IMPORTS AND NO DENO GLOBAL. It is compiled by `tests/at/tsconfig.json`\n *      (`strict`, `skipLibCheck: false`, `types: [\"node\"]`, no DOM library) and it is also run by\n *      Deno inside the edge runtime. A bare `jsr:`/`npm:` specifier or a `Deno` reference would\n *      resolve in neither. The intersection of the two is plain TypeScript over plain data.\n *   2. NO I/O, NO CLOCK, NO RANDOMNESS. Both functions below are pure, so the same judgement can\n *      be replayed by a test with no network and no database.\n *\n * WHICH PROGRAM TYPE-CHECKS IT: the `tests/at` one, and only that one. It reaches that strict\n * program by being imported from `tests/at/suites/req-001/_fixture.ts` and from\n * `tests/at/harness/shipped-verification.selftest.ts`, exactly as `accounts.ts` does, and those\n * imports are the whole of its type coverage.\n *\n * ============================================================================================\n * BOTH FUNCTIONS FAIL CLOSED, AND THAT IS STATED BECAUSE THE EDGE ENTRY POINT HAS NO\n * TYPE-CHECKER.\n * ============================================================================================\n *\n * Measured, not assumed, and the measurement is `accounts.ts`'s: `bun run typecheck` covers the\n * root project (`src/**`) and the `tests/at` program, and neither reaches an edge-function entry\n * point (`supabase/functions/<name>/index.ts`). So a call site that forgets an argument, or that\n * hands over a response body it never checked, is a real possibility rather than a hypothetical\n * one. Every unrecognised shape below therefore reads as UNVERIFIED and every malformed caller is\n * REFUSED. A gate that disappears when a caller is careless is not a gate.\n *\n * The fail-closed promise has a direct oracle rather than only this paragraph:\n * `tests/at/harness/shipped-verification.selftest.ts` drives every malformed shape named here.\n * A promise with no oracle is an untrue stated fact waiting to happen.\n *\n * WHAT THIS MODULE DOES NOT DO: it never sets `email_confirmed_at`, never sends an email, and\n * never follows a link. Verified is Supabase Auth's own fact, written by GoTrue when the emailed\n * link is used. This leaf ships no migration and `public.accounts` gains no column, so there is\n * no second place where \"verified\" is recorded and no two records to disagree.\n *\n * AND IT ASSERTS NOTHING ABOUT LINK EXPIRY, SINGLE USE, OR RESEND. AT-001.11 is retired —\n * \"verification-link expiry/single-use/resend semantics are not stated in REQ-001\"\n * (`.taskmaster/docs/acceptance/at-req-001.md` line 22) — so no code here models those semantics.\n */\n\nimport type { Decision } from './accounts.ts';\n\n/* --------------------------------------------------- 1. is this caller's email address verified */\n\n/**\n * GoTrue's field for the instant a user's email address was confirmed.\n *\n * Named as a constant rather than spelled inline for the reason `github.ts` gives about\n * `identity_data.user_name`: it is a VENDOR's field name, so if it ever turns out to be spelled\n * differently, the change is a one-place change and a reader can find every use of it by\n * searching for one identifier.\n */\nexport const EMAIL_CONFIRMED_AT_FIELD = 'email_confirmed_at';\n\n/**\n * Whether the caller's email address is verified — judged from the GoTrue `/auth/v1/user`\n * response shape and from nothing else.\n *\n * IT TAKES `unknown` ON PURPOSE, exactly as `extractGithubHandle` does. The argument is a JSON\n * body that crossed a network; typing the parameter as a tidy record would be a claim about\n * somebody else's server. So every step down into it is checked here instead.\n *\n * THE ONLY VERIFIED ANSWER is a `email_confirmed_at` that is a string with something in it. A\n * missing field, `null`, an empty or blank string, a number, a boolean, an object, an array, and\n * a user that is not an object at all ALL read as unverified. That list is not defensive\n * decoration — it is the fail-closed direction, and it is the safe one here: the gate below\n * refuses on unverified, so a shape this does not recognise BLOCKS a write loudly instead of\n * waiving the floor quietly.\n *\n * BLANK IS UNVERIFIED, and that is a deliberate reading rather than an accident of using `trim`.\n * A timestamp of three spaces is not an instant, and treating it as one would let the single\n * value most likely to arrive from a broken serialiser mean \"verified\".\n *\n * WHAT IS NOT PROVED BY ANY LOOP-TIER GREEN over this function: that the shape it reads is the\n * shape GoTrue really sends. The fixture renders the canonical shape, which is a prediction of\n * the vendor. The binding measurement is the live proof — `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts`\n * check (d) feeds a REAL `/auth/v1/user` response of a confirmed user to this function.\n */\nexport function emailVerifiedFromUser(user: unknown): boolean {\n  if (typeof user !== 'object' || user === null) return false;\n  const confirmedAt = (user as Record<string, unknown>)[EMAIL_CONFIRMED_AT_FIELD];\n  return typeof confirmedAt === 'string' && confirmedAt.trim() !== '';\n}\n\n/* ------------------------------------------------- 2. may this caller send a Discovery message */\n\n/**\n * WHAT IS TRUE OF THE CALLER, as opposed to what the caller SAID — the same distinction\n * `CompleteSignupCaller` in `./accounts.ts` draws, and for the same security reason.\n *\n * `emailVerified` comes from `emailVerifiedFromUser` over the `/auth/v1/user` response for the\n * authenticated user. It is NEVER a field of the request body: a request field is something a\n * client asserts about itself, and a gate built on one gates nothing.\n */\nexport type DiscoveryMessageCaller = {\n  /** whether Supabase Auth reports this user's email address confirmed */\n  emailVerified: boolean;\n};\n\n/**\n * AT-001.10, as a decision: a Discovery message is permitted to a caller whose email address is\n * verified, and to nobody else.\n *\n * THE ONLY ALLOW IS `caller?.emailVerified === true`. Strict equality against `true`, not a\n * truthiness test: a caller that passed the string `\"false\"`, or `1`, or any other value a\n * careless JSON round trip produces, must not open the gate. This is the same fail-closed posture\n * `validateCompleteSignup` takes on its caller fact, for the same measured reason — the edge\n * entry point has no type-checker, so a missing or malformed argument is a real possibility.\n *\n * THE REFUSAL NAMES EMAIL VERIFICATION TWICE: as what is missing, and as the remedy. AT-001.10's\n * words are \"the attempt is blocked with verification named as the remedy\", and a 403 saying\n * \"forbidden\" would satisfy a weaker reading of that criterion while telling the sender nothing\n * about what to do next. The test body matches the reason against `/verif/i` and `/email/i`.\n *\n * IT IS TYPE-BLIND — see the module header. No account type reaches this function, because\n * decision-8's floor is under EVERY Discovery message and AT-001.10's NGO is one instance of it.\n *\n * IT SAYS NOTHING ABOUT VETTING. Decision-8 also says vetting is \"never the Discovery wall\", so a\n * vetting state is not a second condition hiding in here — verification is the whole floor, and\n * adding anything beside it would be building a wall the ratified text refuses.\n */\nexport function discoveryMessageAllowed(caller: DiscoveryMessageCaller): Decision<'verified'> {\n  if (caller?.emailVerified === true) {\n    return { ok: true, value: 'verified' };\n  }\n  return {\n    ok: false,\n    reason:\n      'a Discovery message needs a verified email address — this account is email-unverified. ' +\n      'Use the verification link sent to the account address, then send the message again',\n  };\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":167,"totalLines":167,"truncated":false}},"title":"supabase\\functions\\_shared\\verification.ts","time":{"start":1786290419750,"end":1786290419757}},"id":"prt_fe734b72f001wl4SSouB84Mmtn","sessionID":"ses_018cbdef8ffeIiDUqdR6dh2IlP","messageID":"msg_fe734a9fc001MDUbAy4Hy0415E"}}
0c389c633b9867b93b1466a77c75d5bc9df66f56:loop/items/AI4DEV-59/artifacts/audit-luna.stderr.log:10251:C:\Users\nirdr\Downloads\ai4good\.claude\worktrees\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\loop\items\AI4DEV-59\artifacts\audit-flash.events.jsonl:30:{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1786290434575,"sessionID":"ses_018cbdef8ffeIiDUqdR6dh2IlP","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_00_axWvrfDUFQWUu3Lq6g3M8628","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\tests\\at\\suites\\req-001\\_fixture.ts"},"output":"<path>C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\tests\\at\\suites\\req-001\\_fixture.ts</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: /**\n2:  * REQ-001's fixture adapter — STORAGE ONLY. Every judgement is the shipped module's.\n3:  *\n4:  * READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING A GREEN FROM THIS SUITE, because the honest claim is narrower than\n5:  * \"REQ-001 works\" and wider than what most acceptance adapters can say.\n6:  *\n7:  * `adapterDerivedCapability()` in `tests/at/harness/capabilities.ts` stamps every `sut.<key>` a\n8:  * STAND-IN unconditionally, and the registry refuses a stand-in above the loop tier. So nothing here\n9:  * can reach the integration-tier run that is the evidence gate. What a loop-tier green from this\n10:  * suite means is exactly this:\n11:  *\n12:  *   - PROVED: the decisions in `supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts`,\n13:  *     `supabase/functions/_shared/github.ts` and `supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts`\n14:  *     behave as the nine acceptance criteria this suite lands require. THE THREE MODULES DO NOT\n15:  *     HAVE THE SAME STANDING, and saying they do would be an untrue stated fact: `accounts.ts` and\n16:  *     `github.ts` are imported by the deployed edge functions, byte for byte the code that ships.\n17:  *     `verification.ts` is imported by NO deployed function — it is the module the FUTURE Discovery\n18:  *     send route must import, and today only this suite and\n19:  *     `tests/at/harness/shipped-verification.selftest.ts` import it. That is decision D-D of the\n20:  *     verification leaf's plan and not an oversight; the module's own header says so too.\n21:  *     Every PRODUCT judgement below — every accept and every refusal about account types, the\n22:  *     GitHub precondition, the verified fact and the Discovery floor — comes from those modules,\n23:  *     and so does the onboarding import's content. THE ONE EXCEPTION is the fixture's own\n24:  *     BOOKKEEPING precondition refusals: `sendDiscoveryMessage` and `createOrganization` refuse an\n25:  *     unknown session and an account that never completed signup, which is this storage checking\n26:  *     that the world it was handed exists rather than any shipped rule judging anything. There is\n27:  *     no second copy of the product rules in this file, deliberately: the moment there is one, this\n28:  *     suite is grading a puppet and the green is worth nothing.\n29:  *   - NOT PROVED: that the migration is correct, that either edge function works, that row-level\n30:  *     security denies what it should, that Supabase Auth is configured, or that Google or GitHub\n31:  *     sign-in works. None of that is reachable from here — the storage below is a Map. The evidence\n32:  *     for that half at THIS tree's head is `loop/items/AI4DEV-58/proof-local.txt`, produced against\n33:  *     the live local stack on one machine and EXISTS: 9 checks, 8 passed, 0 failed, 1 skipped. It\n34:  *     covers the migration, the GitHub gate, the imported profile, the database's own backstops\n35:  *     against a caller who never met TypeScript, atomicity, and the provider configuration. THE\n36:  *     SKIP IS THE GITHUB HANDSHAKE: no GitHub OAuth app exists for this project, so \"GitHub\n37:  *     sign-in works\" is not proved by the live tier either. The GOOGLE HANDSHAKE is unproved for\n38:  *     the same reason and was never re-attempted here — no Google credential was in the\n39:  *     environment when the predecessor measured it and none is now. No amount of green anywhere in\n40:  *     this suite proves either handshake. A reviewer cannot reproduce that transcript; it is one\n41:  *     machine's word.\n42:  *\n43:  *     `loop/items/AI4DEV-57/proof-local.txt` is retained for the ONE thing only it still covers:\n44:  *     `create-organization`, which was exercised there and is untouched by this leaf. Its\n45:  *     completion-path and schema evidence is SUPERSEDED — it predates this migration and called a\n46:  *     `complete_signup` that no longer exists.\n47:  *\n48:  * WHAT THE STORAGE HALF DOES MIRROR, because a stand-in that mirrors nothing tests nothing: one\n49:  * account row per auth user (so \"one account holds exactly one global type\" is structural here as it\n50:  * is in the schema, not a rule the adapter remembers), and completion is all-or-nothing (so a body\n51:  * asserting no partial state is asserting the same shape the database's `complete_signup` function\n52:  * guarantees). It mirrors the SHAPE; the database's own guarantee is proved on the live stack.\n53:  *\n54:  * ============================================================================================\n55:  * THE VENDOR MIRRORS, EACH WITH WHAT BINDS IT — the verification leaf's four, named one by one.\n56:  * ============================================================================================\n57:  *\n58:  * A MIRROR IS NOT A PRODUCT JUDGEMENT, and keeping the two apart is what this section is for. The\n59:  * two PRODUCT judgements about verification — whether an address is verified, and whether an\n60:  * unverified caller may send a Discovery message — come from the shipped\n61:  * `supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts` and from nowhere else; there is no second copy of\n62:  * either rule in this file. What is left is a prediction of what Supabase Auth DOES, and a\n63:  * prediction has to say what would prove it wrong.\n64:  *\n65:  *   1. AN EMAIL/PASSWORD REGISTRATION STARTS UNCONFIRMED AND AN EMAIL CARRYING A VERIFICATION\n66:  *      LINK IS SENT.\n67:  *      BOUND — checks (a) and (b) of `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts` on the live stack: the\n68:  *      signup's own wire response, `auth.users.email_confirmed_at IS NULL` read on the database,\n69:  *      and the confirmation email held by the local mail catcher.\n70:  *   2. A LINK THAT WAS NEVER ISSUED CONFIRMS NOTHING.\n71:  *      BOUND — check (b2) of `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts`, and by that check alone. It\n72:  *      follows a TAMPERED variant of the real link — the token mutated, so a token GoTrue never\n73:  *      issued — BEFORE the real link is followed, and reads `auth.users.email_confirmed_at` still\n74:  *      NULL afterwards. This entry used to cite checks (a)-(d), which measure only the POSITIVE\n75:  *      half; two blind reviewers found that overstatement independently, so the check that\n76:  *      measures the negative was added rather than the label softened. Note what this is NOT: it\n77:  *      is not a claim about expiry, single use or resend. The token followed was never minted, so\n78:  *      no lifetime and no use count is in play. AT-001.11 is retired and none of those semantics\n79:  *      is modelled here.\n80:  *   3. USING THE EMAILED LINK SETS `email_confirmed_at`.\n81:  *      BOUND — checks (b) and (d): the link is followed by HTTP GET and the column flips to\n82:  *      non-null, after which the real `/auth/v1/user` response is fed to the SHIPPED\n83:  *      `emailVerifiedFromUser`, which answers true.\n84:  *   4. A PROVIDER REGISTRATION (Google, GitHub) STARTS CONFIRMED, because the provider vouched for\n85:  *      the address.\n86:  *      **UNBOUND, AND SAID PLAINLY.** No OAuth app or credential exists in this environment, so no\n87:  *      live provider session is obtainable — the same recorded gap that leaves the OAuth handshake\n88:  *      itself unproved at every tier, stated in this header since the predecessor item. This item\n89:  *      binds it nowhere and claims nothing about it. NO TEST IN THIS SUITE READS A PROVIDER USER'S\n90:  *      VERIFIED STATE: both verification bodies register by email and password. If the prediction\n91:  *      is wrong — a real provider user arriving unconfirmed — the shipped gate FAILS CLOSED: it\n92:  *      refuses that caller, never allows one. The mirror is bound by the first item that ships a\n93:  *      real provider-path consumer, and not before.\n94:  *\n95:  * THE PROVISIONED PLATFORM ADMINISTRATOR STARTS CONFIRMED, and it is a mirror of a RECIPE rather\n96:  * than of a live measurement. `provisionPlatformAdmin` below marks its auth user confirmed and\n97:  * mints no verification link, because creating an administrator sends no email. The recipe it\n98:  * mirrors is the only one this repository records: the predecessor item's live-proof script creates\n99:  * that user through `POST /auth/v1/admin/users` with `email_confirm: true`. The earlier version of\n100:  * this fixture let the administrator start UNCONFIRMED by reusing the public email path, which\n101:  * contradicted that record.\n102:  * WHAT BINDS IT: nothing yet, and that is said plainly. NOTHING READS AN ADMINISTRATOR'S VERIFIED\n103:  * STATE — not AT-001.07, not anything else in this suite. The predecessor's transcript ran with\n104:  * confirmations OFF, so it does not measure the column under this leaf's flip either. So this\n105:  * mirror is labelled by its recipe and is never called live-bound; the first item that reads an\n106:  * administrator's verified state is the one that binds it.\n107:  *\n108:  * AND THE DISCOVERY SEND SURFACE IS NOT A MIRROR AT ALL — it mirrors nothing, because there is\n109:  * nothing to mirror. No Discovery route exists in this repository at either tier; it is\n110:  * REQ-002/004's. `sendDiscoveryMessage` below is a stand-in surface whose whole job is to put the\n111:  * shipped gate on a tested path. No green over it says anything about enforcement anywhere.\n112:  */\n113: \n114: import type { FixtureWorld, FixtureWorldStore } from '../../harness/fixtures.ts';\n115: import {\n116:   PLATFORM_ACKNOWLEDGMENT_KIND,\n117:   PUBLIC_SIGNUP_ACCOUNT_TYPES,\n118:   ngoOnlyActionAllowed,\n119:   validateCompleteSignup,\n120:   validateOrganizationName,\n121:   type AccountType,\n122:   type CompleteSignupRequest,\n123: } from '../../../../supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts';\n124: // BOTH shipped GitHub judgements, not one. The IMPORT SOURCE is the shipped stub, not a copy living\n125: // in this file — AT-001.05 compares the profile it reads back against `stubGithubStatsFor`, so if the\n126: // two were separate implementations the test would grade the fixture's copy and say nothing about\n127: // what the edge function writes. And `extractGithubHandle` is the OTHER shipped decision on this\n128: // path: `completeSignup` below derives the caller fact through it rather than reading the stored\n129: // handle straight, exactly as `resolveCaller` does in the edge function. Before that, a regression\n130: // in the extractor — returning null for a linked identity — would have rejected every linked\n131: // volunteer at the deployed edge while this suite stayed green.\n132: import { extractGithubHandle, stubGithubStatsFor } from '../../../../supabase/functions/_shared/github.ts';\n133: // BOTH shipped verification judgements, for the same reason both GitHub ones are imported above.\n134: // `emailVerifiedFromUser` is how the verified fact is DERIVED from the rendered GoTrue user shape\n135: // on every read — never read straight off storage — and `discoveryMessageAllowed` is the ONLY thing\n136: // that decides whether a Discovery send is refused. Between them there is no rule about\n137: // verification left in this file to drift.\n138: import {\n139:   discoveryMessageAllowed,\n140:   emailVerifiedFromUser,\n141: } from '../../../../supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts';\n142: import type {\n143:   AccountRow,\n144:   AccountsSut,\n145:   AcknowledgmentRow,\n146:   CompleteSignupOutcome,\n147:   CreateOrganizationOutcome,\n148:   MembershipRow,\n149:   OrganizationRow,\n150:   SendDiscoveryMessageOutcome,\n151:   Session,\n152:   SessionProvider,\n153:   SignInOutcome,\n154:   VolunteerProfileRow,\n155:   World,\n156: } from './_contract.ts';\n157: \n158: /**\n159:  * WHICH REQUIREMENT THIS ADAPTER IS, declared by the adapter itself.\n160:  *\n161:  * `harness/suite-adapters.ts` constrains its map entry to match this literal, and `loadAdapter()` in\n162:  * `harness/index.ts` re-checks it against the requirement it was asked for. Without it the\n163:  * type-check could describe one suite while the run drove another.\n164:  */\n165: export const requirement = 'req-001' as const;\n166: \n167: interface AdapterOptions {\n168:   worlds: FixtureWorldStore;\n169: }\n170: \n171: /** An auth user as Supabase Auth holds one: credentials and the provider that established them. */\n172: interface AuthUser {\n173:   id: string;\n174:   email: string;\n175:   password: string | null;\n176:   provider: SessionProvider;\n177:   /**\n178:    * The handle of a LINKED GitHub identity, or null — Auth's `identities[]` narrowed to the one\n179:    * field anything here reads.\n180:    *\n181:    * It is stored on the auth user and NOT on the session, which is the whole reason the gate is a\n182:    * caller fact: linking happens after a session exists, changes nothing about how that session was\n183:    * established, and is a property of the user Auth answers for.\n184:    */\n185:   githubHandle: string | null;\n186:   /**\n187:    * GoTrue's `email_confirmed_at`, or null while the address is unconfirmed — vendor mirror 1 and\n188:    * 4 in this file's header.\n189:    *\n190:    * IT IS STORED AS THE VENDOR'S OWN FIELD VALUE, a nullable instant, and never as a boolean. The\n191:    * shipped `emailVerifiedFromUser` is what turns it into an answer, and it does so from the\n192:    * rendered `/auth/v1/user` shape rather than from this field directly. Storing a boolean here\n193:    * would put the judgement in storage, which is the one thing this file's opening paragraph\n194:    * promises it does not do.\n195:    */\n196:   emailConfirmedAt: string | null;\n197:   /**\n198:    * The verification link Auth emailed for this address, or null when no confirmation email was\n199:    * sent — the fixture's stand-in for the message the local mail catcher holds on the live stack.\n200:    *\n201:    * IT IS NOT CLEARED WHEN IT IS USED, deliberately. Clearing it would model SINGLE USE, and\n202:    * single-use semantics are retired AT-001.11's — \"not stated in REQ-001\". The fixture models\n203:    * what the criterion states and no more.\n204:    */\n205:   verificationLink: string | null;\n206: }\n207: \n208: interface StoredAcknowledgment extends AcknowledgmentRow {}\n209: \n210: interface State {\n211:   authUsers: Map<string, AuthUser>;\n212:   /** email -> auth user id, because sign-in arrives with an address rather than an id */\n213:   byEmail: Map<string, string>;\n214:   /**\n215:    * verification link -> auth user id, because a link arrives on its own with no address beside it\n216:    * — which is exactly the situation on the live stack, where the link is followed from an inbox.\n217:    *\n218:    * A link this map does not hold was never issued, and confirms nothing.\n219:    */\n220:   byVerificationLink: Map<string, string>;\n221:   accounts: Map<string, AccountRow>;\n222:   organizations: Map<string, OrganizationRow>;\n223:   /** `${organizationId}:${accountId}` -> row */\n224:   memberships: Map<string, MembershipRow>;\n225:   acknowledgments: StoredAcknowledgment[];\n226:   /** account id -> the imported volunteer profile, mirroring `public.volunteer_profiles`'s primary key */\n227:   volunteerProfiles: Map<string, VolunteerProfileRow>;\n228:   /**\n229:    * account id -> the Discovery message bodies that account has sent.\n230:    *\n231:    * There is NO table behind this and no route that writes one; see the header's closing paragraph.\n232:    * It exists so AT-001.10 can assert that a refused send wrote NOTHING, which the refusal's own\n233:    * return value cannot show.\n234:    */\n235:   discoveryMessages: Map<string, string[]>;\n236:   nextId: number;\n237: }\n238: \n239: function membershipKey(organizationId: string, accountId: string): string {\n240:   return `${organizationId}:${accountId}`;\n241: }\n242: \n243: function clone<T>(value: T): T {\n244:   return structuredClone(value);\n245: }\n246: \n247: class AccountsFixtureWorld implements World {\n248:   constructor(\n249:     private readonly base: FixtureWorld,\n250:     private readonly serial: number,\n251:   ) {}\n252: \n253:   email(local: string): string {\n254:     return `${local}+w${this.serial}@example.test`;\n255:   }\n256: \n257:   async teardown(): Promise<void> {\n258:     await this.base.teardown();\n259:   }\n260: }\n261: \n262: export function createFixtureAdapter({ worlds }: AdapterOptions) {\n263:   const state: State = {\n264:     authUsers: new Map(),\n265:     byEmail: new Map(),\n266:     byVerificationLink: new Map(),\n267:     accounts: new Map(),\n268:     organizations: new Map(),\n269:     memberships: new Map(),\n270:     acknowledgments: [],\n271:     volunteerProfiles: new Map(),\n272:     discoveryMessages: new Map(),\n273:     nextId: 1,\n274:   };\n275:   const openedWorlds = new Set<AccountsFixtureWorld>();\n276:   let worldSerial = 0;\n277: \n278:   const nextId = (prefix: string): string => `${prefix}-${state.nextId++}`;\n279: \n280:   /**\n281:    * The instant a confirmation is recorded at.\n282:    *\n283:    * Fixed rather than read from a clock, for the same reason the acknowledgment's instant is: no\n284:    * criterion here depends on WHICH instant, only on whether one is present, and a real timestamp\n285:    * would make the capture non-deterministic for no gain. The shipped `emailVerifiedFromUser`\n286:    * judges presence and never parses the value, so this is the whole of what the field has to be.\n287:    */\n288:   const CONFIRMED_AT = '2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z';\n289: \n290:   const register = (\n291:     email: string,\n292:     password: string | null,\n293:     provider: SessionProvider,\n294:     githubHandle: string | null = null,\n295:     /**\n296:      * Whether the CREATOR already confirmed the address, which only an operator can do.\n297:      *\n298:      * It exists for `provisionPlatformAdmin` and for nothing else. See the header: the recipe this\n299:      * repository records creates an administrator through `POST /auth/v1/admin/users` with\n300:      * `email_confirm: true`, so that user never receives a confirmation email and never has a link.\n301:      */\n302:     confirmedByTheCreator = false,\n303:   ): Session => {\n304:     // Supabase Auth's own uniqueness, mirrored: a second registration on one address is not a new\n305:     // user. Left out, a body could accidentally create two auth users for one address and then read\n306:     // back \"one account per user\" from a situation the real system cannot be in.\n307:     const existing = state.byEmail.get(email);\n308:     if (existing) throw new Error(`fixture: ${email} is already registered — Supabase Auth would refuse this`);\n309: \n310:     // VENDOR MIRRORS 1 AND 4, and the header names what binds each. A public email/password\n311:     // registration starts UNCONFIRMED and Auth emails a confirmation link. A registration a\n312:     // provider vouched for, and one an operator created with the address already confirmed, both\n313:     // start CONFIRMED and receive no confirmation email, so neither has a link. The provider half\n314:     // is declared UNBOUND in the header — no OAuth credential exists here — and nothing in this\n315:     // suite reads a provider user's verified state.\n316:     const id = nextId('user');\n317:     const confirmedAtCreation = provider !== 'email' || confirmedByTheCreator;\n318:     const emailConfirmedAt = confirmedAtCreation ? CONFIRMED_AT : null;\n319:     // Derived from the user id rather than from a second counter, so the link is unique by\n320:     // construction and a link read in a failure message says which user it belongs to.\n321:     const verificationLink = confirmedAtCreation ? null : `verify-${id}`;\n322: \n323:     const user: AuthUser = { id, email, password, provider, githubHandle, emailConfirmedAt, verificationLink };\n324:     state.authUsers.set(user.id, user);\n325:     state.byEmail.set(email, user.id);\n326:     if (verificationLink !== null) state.byVerificationLink.set(verificationLink, user.id);\n327:     return { accountId: user.id, email: user.email, provider: user.provider };\n328:   };\n329: \n330:   /**\n331:    * The stored auth state rendered as the canonical GoTrue `/auth/v1/user` response shape.\n332:    *\n333:    * ONE RENDERER FOR BOTH SHIPPED EXTRACTORS. `extractGithubHandle` reads `identities[]` and\n334:    * `emailVerifiedFromUser` reads `email_confirmed_at`, and both read them off the SAME object here\n335:    * — which is the point: on the live stack they read one response body, so rendering two different\n336:    * shapes for them would be a divergence this suite could never notice.\n337:    *\n338:    * WHAT IS STILL NOT PROVED by anything that calls this: that this shape is the shape GoTrue really\n339:    * sends. That is a prediction of the vendor. The live proof is what binds it.\n340:    */\n341:   const renderAuthUser = (user: AuthUser): Record<string, unknown> => ({\n342:     id: user.id,\n343:     email: user.email,\n344:     email_confirmed_at: user.emailConfirmedAt,\n345:     identities:\n346:       user.githubHandle === null\n347:         ? []\n348:         : [{ provider: 'github', identity_data: { user_name: user.githubHandle } }],\n349:   });\n350: \n351:   /**\n352:    * Which providers can sign this user back in — Auth's `identities[]`, reduced to what AT-001.02\n353:    * asks about.\n354:    *\n355:    * A GitHub signup and a later GitHub LINK produce the same answer here, deliberately: after either,\n356:    * the account carries a GitHub identity, and \"a later sign-in via GitHub returns to the same\n357:    * account\" is a statement about the identity, not about which provider happened to create the user.\n358:    */\n359:   const reachableThroughProvider = (user: AuthUser, provider: SessionProvider): boolean =>\n360:     provider === 'github' ? user.githubHandle !== null : user.provider === provider;\n361: \n362:   /**\n363:    * The `complete-signup` operation, at the loop tier.\n364:    *\n365:    * EVERY DECISION ON THIS PATH COMES FROM THE SHIPPED MODULE. What is left here is the four writes,\n366:    * performed together or not at all — which is the shape `public.complete_signup` guarantees inside\n367:    * one transaction on the real database, and which step 7 of the plan proves there.\n368:    */\n369:   const completeSignup = async (\n370:     session: Session,\n371:     request: CompleteSignupRequest,\n372:     ip: string,\n373:   ): Promise<CompleteSignupOutcome> => {\n374:     const authUser = state.authUsers.get(session.accountId);\n375:     if (!authUser) {\n376:       return { ok: false, reason: 'no authenticated user — sign in before completing signup' };\n377:     }\n378: \n379:     // THE CALLER FACT COMES FROM THE STORED AUTH USER, never from the request. That is the same\n380:     // shape the edge function has, where it comes from `/auth/v1/user` — a client cannot assert it\n381:     // in either place, which is the whole security property of the volunteer gate.\n382:     //\n383:     // AND IT IS DERIVED, NOT READ. The stored state is rendered as the canonical GoTrue\n384:     // `/auth/v1/user` shape — an `identities[]` array, empty when nothing is linked — and the handle\n385:     // is then judged out of it by the SHIPPED `extractGithubHandle`, which is precisely what\n386:     // `resolveCaller` does with the real response. Passing `authUser.githubHandle` in directly, as\n387:     // this used to, pre-narrowed the fact and left the extractor unexecuted by any test: storage\n388:     // would have been doing a judgement's job, which is the one thing this file's opening paragraph\n389:     // promises it does not do. What is still NOT proved here is GoTrue's real serialisation — that\n390:     // this shape is the shape Auth sends. Only the live proof touches that.\n391:     //\n392:     // The rendering itself is `renderAuthUser` above, shared with the verified-fact read, because\n393:     // on the live stack both facts come out of ONE response body and two renderings here could\n394:     // diverge with nothing able to notice.\n395:     const callerUser = renderAuthUser(authUser);\n396:     const decision = validateCompleteSignup(request, { githubHandle: extractGithubHandle(callerUser) });\n397:     if (!decision.ok) return { ok: false, reason: decision.reason };\n398:     const { accountType, organizationName, acknowledgmentTextVersion, githubHandle } = decision.value;\n399: \n400:     // ONE ROW PER AUTH USER is what makes \"one account holds exactly one global type\" structural\n401:     // rather than remembered — the schema states it as a primary key, and this states it as a\n402:     // refusal at the same point.\n403:     if (state.accounts.has(session.accountId)) {\n404:       return { ok: false, reason: 'this account has already completed signup — one account holds exactly one global type' };\n405:     }\n406: \n407:     const account: AccountRow = { id: session.accountId, accountType };\n408:     let organization: OrganizationRow | null = null;\n409:     let membership: MembershipRow | null = null;\n410: \n411:     if (organizationName !== null) {\n412:       organization = { id: nextId('org'), name: organizationName };\n413:       membership = { organizationId: organization.id, accountId: account.id, role: 'admin' };\n414:     }\n415: \n416:     // THE ONBOARDING IMPORT, COMPUTED HERE AND WRITTEN BELOW WITH EVERYTHING ELSE — which is the\n417:     // shape `public.complete_signup` guarantees inside one transaction on the real database. There\n418:     // is no queue and no deferred job: a volunteer completion either lands the account AND the\n419:     // populated profile, or lands nothing. AT-001.05's \"a queued-but-empty import fails this test\"\n420:     // is unrepresentable rather than merely untested.\n421:     let volunteerProfile: VolunteerProfileRow | null = null;\n422:     if (githubHandle !== null) {\n423:       const stats = stubGithubStatsFor(githubHandle);\n424:       volunteerProfile = {\n425:         accountId: session.accountId,\n426:         githubHandle,\n427:         topLanguages: stats.topLanguages,\n428:         repositoryCount: stats.repositoryCount,\n429:         contributionSummary: stats.contributionSummary,\n430:         // Fixed rather than read from a clock, for the same reason the acknowledgment's instant is:\n431:         // nothing in these criteria depends on WHICH instant, and the live-stack proof reads the\n432:         // database's own `now()`.\n433:         importedAt: '2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z',\n434:       };\n435:     }\n436: \n437:     const acknowledgment: StoredAcknowledgment = {\n438:       accountId: account.id,\n439:       kind: PLATFORM_ACKNOWLEDGMENT_KIND,\n440:       // Fixed rather than read from a clock: nothing in this leaf's four criteria depends on WHICH\n441:       // instant it is, only that one is recorded, and a real timestamp would make the capture\n442:       // non-deterministic for no gain. The live-stack proof reads `now()` from the database.\n443:       acknowledgedAt: '2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z',\n444:       ip,\n445:       textVersion: acknowledgmentTextVersion,\n446:     };\n447: \n448:     // All of them, together. Nothing above this line has mutated `state`.\n449:     state.accounts.set(account.id, account);\n450:     if (organization) state.organizations.set(organization.id, organization);\n451:     if (membership) state.memberships.set(membershipKey(membership.organizationId, membership.accountId), membership);\n452:     if (volunteerProfile) state.volunteerProfiles.set(volunteerProfile.accountId, volunteerProfile);\n453:     state.acknowledgments.push(acknowledgment);\n454: \n455:     return { ok: true, accountId: account.id, organizationId: organization?.id ?? null };\n456:   };\n457: \n458:   const sut: AccountsSut = {\n459:     // Read straight off the shipped constant. A literal here would be a second statement of the same\n460:     // fact, and AT-001.07 would then be asserting what this file says rather than what ships.\n461:     publicSignupAccountTypes: async () => [...PUBLIC_SIGNUP_ACCOUNT_TYPES],\n462: \n463:     registerWithEmailPassword: async (email, password) => register(email, password, 'email'),\n464:     registerWithProvider: async (provider, email) => register(email, null, provider),\n465:     // A GitHub signup links the GitHub identity it signed up with — that is what makes it a GitHub\n466:     // signup rather than a session that merely says 'github'. Both halves are recorded here in one\n467:     // step because Auth performs them in one round trip.\n468:     registerWithGithub: async (email, githubHandle) => register(email, null, 'github', githubHandle),\n469: \n470:     linkGithubIdentity: async (session, githubHandle) => {\n471:       const user = state.authUsers.get(session.accountId);\n472:       // A throw, not a refusal: a body that links an identity onto a user Auth never registered has\n473:       // a bug in the TEST, and returning a polite outcome would let that bug read as a product\n474:       // refusal further down.\n475:       if (!user) throw new Error(`fixture: no auth user ${session.accountId} to link a GitHub identity to`);\n476:       user.githubHandle = githubHandle;\n477:     },\n478: \n479:     signInWithEmailPassword: async (email, password): Promise<SignInOutcome> => {\n480:       const userId = state.byEmail.get(email);\n481:       const user = userId ? state.authUsers.get(userId) : undefined;\n482:       // One reason for both branches: telling \"no such account\" apart from \"wrong password\" is an\n483:       // existence oracle, and REQ-001 spends AT-001.21 forbidding exactly that shape elsewhere.\n484:       if (!user || user.password === null || user.password !== password) {\n485:         return { ok: false, reason: 'the email or password is incorrect' };\n486:       }\n487:       return { ok: true, session: { accountId: user.id, email: user.email, provider: user.provider } };\n488:     },\n489: \n490:     signInWithProvider: async (provider, email): Promise<SignInOutcome> => {\n491:       const userId = state.byEmail.get(email);\n492:       const user = userId ? state.authUsers.get(userId) : undefined;\n493:       // One reason again, for the AT-001.21 reason above: \"no such account\" and \"that provider is\n494:       // not linked to it\" must not be distinguishable to a caller.\n495:       if (!user || !reachableThroughProvider(user, provider)) {\n496:         return { ok: false, reason: `no account of this address is reachable through ${provider}` };\n497:       }\n498:       // THE SAME ACCOUNT, which is the clause under test. The id comes from the stored user, so a\n499:       // second identity minted per sign-in would show up as a different `accountId` rather than\n500:       // being invisible.\n501:       return { ok: true, session: { accountId: user.id, email: user.email, provider } };\n502:     },\n503: \n504:     // DERIVED THROUGH THE SHIPPED EXTRACTOR, never read off storage. `user.emailConfirmedAt` is\n505:     // rendered into the canonical `/auth/v1/user` shape and `emailVerifiedFromUser` judges it —\n506:     // the same R3 pattern `completeSignup` uses for the GitHub handle, and for the same reason:\n507:     // the extractor is what the future Discovery route will run, so it has to sit on the tested\n508:     // path rather than being a function no test ever executes.\n509:     emailVerified: async (accountId) => {\n510:       const user = state.authUsers.get(accountId);\n511:       // A throw, not `false`. `false` is the answer for an unverified user, so returning it here\n512:       // would make a body that read a user Auth never registered look like a correct negative —\n513:       // and the whole discriminating pair in AT-001.09 turns on telling those two apart.\n514:       if (!user) throw new Error(`fixture: no auth user ${accountId} whose verified state could be read`);\n515:       return emailVerifiedFromUser(renderAuthUser(user));\n516:     },\n517: \n518:     emailedVerificationLink: async (email) => {\n519:       const userId = state.byEmail.get(email);\n520:       const user = userId ? state.authUsers.get(userId) : undefined;\n521:       return user?.verificationLink ?? null;\n522:     },\n523: \n524:     // VENDOR MIRRORS 2 AND 3 — the header names what binds each. Using the link Auth emailed\n525:     // confirms that address; a link that was never issued flips nothing and says so.\n526:     //\n527:     // NOTHING HERE MODELS EXPIRY, SINGLE USE OR RESEND. The link is NOT removed from the map after\n528:     // it is used, because removing it would be single-use semantics, and AT-001.11 — which is\n529:     // where those semantics lived — is retired as unstated in REQ-001.\n530:     useVerificationLink: async (link) => {\n531:       const userId = state.byVerificationLink.get(link);\n532:       const user = userId ? state.authUsers.get(userId) : undefined;\n533:       if (!user) return { ok: false };\n534:       user.emailConfirmedAt = CONFIRMED_AT;\n535:       return { ok: true };\n536:     },\n537: \n538:     completeSignup,\n539: \n540:     // THE STAND-IN SURFACE FOR A ROUTE THAT DOES NOT EXIST — see the header's closing paragraph\n541:     // and `AccountsSut`'s fourth kind. Every judgement below is the shipped module's; what is left\n542:     // here is one refusal about bookkeeping and one write.\n543:     sendDiscoveryMessage: async (session, body): Promise<SendDiscoveryMessageOutcome> => {\n544:       const authUser = state.authUsers.get(session.accountId);\n545:       if (!authUser) return { ok: false, reason: 'no authenticated user — sign in before sending a Discovery message' };\n546:       // The same bookkeeping refusal `createOrganization` gives, and it is THIS FILE'S, not a\n547:       // product rule: an account that has not completed signup has no sender to record against.\n548:       // It is checked FIRST so that AT-001.10's refusal is unambiguously the gate's own — the\n549:       // account there has completed signup, so this branch cannot be what answers.\n550:       if (!state.accounts.has(session.accountId)) {\n551:         return { ok: false, reason: 'complete signup before sending a Discovery message' };\n552:       }\n553: \n554:       // THE VERIFIED FACT IS A CALLER FACT, derived exactly as `emailVerified` derives it: from the\n555:       // rendered response shape, through the shipped extractor. It is never a request field —\n556:       // a request field is something a client asserts about itself, and a floor built on one is no\n557:       // floor at all.\n558:       const emailVerified = emailVerifiedFromUser(renderAuthUser(authUser));\n559: \n560:       // THE ONLY DECISION ON THIS PATH, and it is the shipped one. The refusal text is the GATE's,\n561:       // carried through unchanged — AT-001.10 matches the reason, so restating it here would make\n562:       // the test grade this file instead of the module that ships.\n563:       const allowed = discoveryMessageAllowed({ emailVerified });\n564:       if (!allowed.ok) return { ok: false, reason: allowed.reason };\n565: \n566:       // ONLY REACHED ON ALLOW, so a refusal writes nothing — which is what `discoveryMessagesBy`\n567:       // is read for. The body is stored opaquely: recipients, threads and message state are\n568:       // REQ-002/004's semantics and none of them is invented here.\n569:       const sent = state.discoveryMessages.get(session.accountId) ?? [];\n570:       sent.push(body);\n571:       state.discoveryMessages.set(session.accountId, sent);\n572:       return { ok: true };\n573:     },\n574: \n575:     discoveryMessagesBy: async (accountId) => clone(state.discoveryMessages.get(accountId) ?? []),\n576: \n577:     createOrganization: async (session, organizationName): Promise<CreateOrganizationOutcome> => {\n578:       const account = state.accounts.get(session.accountId);\n579:       if (!account) return { ok: false, reason: 'complete signup before creating an organisation' };\n580: \n581:       // THE REFUSAL IS THE SHIPPED MODULE'S, not this file's. That is what makes AT-001.06 a test of\n582:       // an application boundary rather than of a helper called directly from a test body.\n583:       const allowed = ngoOnlyActionAllowed(account.accountType);\n584:       if (!allowed.ok) return { ok: false, reason: allowed.reason };\n585: \n586:       // The name rule is the shipped module's too. It used to be spelled out here, which made this\n587:       // file hold a second copy of a rule — the one thing its opening paragraph promises it does\n588:       // not do.\n589:       const name = validateOrganizationName(organizationName);\n590:       if (!name.ok) return { ok: false, reason: name.reason };\n591: \n592:       const organization: OrganizationRow = { id: nextId('org'), name: name.value };\n593:       const membership: MembershipRow = { organizationId: organization.id, accountId: account.id, role: 'admin' };\n594:       state.organizations.set(organization.id, organization);\n595:       state.memberships.set(membershipKey(organization.id, account.id), membership);\n596:       return { ok: true, organizationId: organization.id };\n597:     },\n598: \n599:     account: async (accountId) => clone(state.accounts.get(accountId) ?? null),\n600:     organization: async (organizationId) => clone(state.organizations.get(organizationId) ?? null),\n601:     membership: async (organizationId, accountId) => clone(state.memberships.get(membershipKey(organizationId, accountId)) ?? null),\n602:     acknowledgments: async (accountId) => clone(state.acknowledgments.filter((row) => row.accountId === accountId)),\n603:     volunteerProfile: async (accountId) => clone(state.volunteerProfiles.get(accountId) ?? null),\n604: \n605:     // The two searches a refused action needs: it hands back no identifier, so the rows it must NOT\n606:     // have written can only be looked for by the name that was attempted and by who holds what.\n607:     organizationsNamed: async (name) => clone([...state.organizations.values()].filter((row) => row.name === name.trim())),\n608:     membershipsOf: async (accountId) => clone([...state.memberships.values()].filter((row) => row.accountId === accountId)),\n609: \n610:     // DISCRIMINATING — and WHICH predicate that green grades is worth being exact about, because\n611:     // this one is not the shipped one. `public.has_platform_acknowledgment` is SQL, and a\n612:     // TypeScript module cannot supply a SQL predicate; the one judgement inside it — which\n613:     // acknowledgment kind counts — is shared, which is why the constant below is imported rather\n614:     // than spelled. So AT-001.01's loop-tier green proves THE RULE and THIS storage: a completion\n615:     // writes the row, and the answer is false before and true after. The shipped SQL predicate\n616:     // could still return true unconditionally and this would stay green. What proves that one is\n617:     // step 7(h) of the plan, against the live database, and nothing else in this item.\n618:     hasPlatformAcknowledgment: async (accountId) =>\n619:       state.acknowledgments.some((row) => row.accountId === accountId && row.kind === PLATFORM_ACKNOWLEDGMENT_KIND),\n620: \n621:     provisionPlatformAdmin: async (email, password) => {\n622:       // CONFIRMED AT PROVISIONING, AND NO VERIFICATION LINK — the header's administrator paragraph\n623:       // says why: the only provisioning recipe this repository records creates the user with\n624:       // `email_confirm: true`, and creating an administrator sends no email.\n625:       const session = register(email, password, 'email', null, true);\n626:       // Written directly, bypassing `completeSignup` — which is not a shortcut but the point:\n627:       // `parseAccountType` refuses this type, so the public path CANNOT produce it, and the only way\n628:       // an administrator exists is an authority the public never holds. The type is still taken from\n629:       // the shipped vocabulary rather than spelled as a literal.\n630:       const accountType: AccountType = 'platform_admin';\n631:       state.accounts.set(session.accountId, { id: session.accountId, accountType });\n632:       return session;\n633:     },\n634:   };\n635: \n636:   return {\n637:     sut: { accounts: sut },\n638:     fixtures: {\n639:       world: async (name: string) => {\n640:         const base = await worlds.world(name);\n641:         const world = new AccountsFixtureWorld(base, ++worldSerial);\n642:         openedWorlds.add(world);\n643:         return world;\n644:       },\n645:     },\n646:     teardown: async () => {\n647:       await Promise.all([...openedWorlds].map((world) => world.teardown()));\n648:       openedWorlds.clear();\n649:       state.authUsers.clear();\n650:       state.byEmail.clear();\n651:       state.byVerificationLink.clear();\n652:       state.accounts.clear();\n653:       state.organizations.clear();\n654:       state.memberships.clear();\n655:       state.acknowledgments.length = 0;\n656:       state.volunteerProfiles.clear();\n657:       state.discoveryMessages.clear();\n658:       state.nextId = 1;\n659:     },\n660:   };\n661: }\n\n(End of file - total 661 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"/**\n * REQ-001's fixture adapter — STORAGE ONLY. Every judgement is the shipped module's.\n *\n * READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING A GREEN FROM THIS SUITE, because the honest claim is narrower than\n * \"REQ-001 works\" and wider than what most acceptance adapters can say.\n *\n * `adapterDerivedCapability()` in `tests/at/harness/capabilities.ts` stamps every `sut.<key>` a\n * STAND-IN unconditionally, and the registry refuses a stand-in above the loop tier. So nothing here\n * can reach the integration-tier run that is the evidence gate. What a loop-tier green from this\n * suite means is exactly this:\n *\n *   - PROVED: the decisions in `supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts`,\n *     `supabase/functions/_shared/github.ts` and `supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts`\n *     behave as the nine acceptance criteria this suite lands require. THE THREE MODULES DO NOT\n *     HAVE THE SAME STANDING, and saying they do would be an untrue stated fact: `accounts.ts` and\n *     `github.ts` are imported by the deployed edge functions, byte for byte the code that ships.\n *     `verification.ts` is imported by NO deployed function — it is the module the FUTURE Discovery\n *     send route must import, and today only this suite and\n *     `tests/at/harness/shipped-verification.selftest.ts` import it. That is decision D-D of the\n *     verification leaf's plan and not an oversight; the module's own header says so too.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\tests\\at\\suites\\req-001\\_fixture.ts","text":"/**\n * REQ-001's fixture adapter — STORAGE ONLY. Every judgement is the shipped module's.\n *\n * READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING A GREEN FROM THIS SUITE, because the honest claim is narrower than\n * \"REQ-001 works\" and wider than what most acceptance adapters can say.\n *\n * `adapterDerivedCapability()` in `tests/at/harness/capabilities.ts` stamps every `sut.<key>` a\n * STAND-IN unconditionally, and the registry refuses a stand-in above the loop tier. So nothing here\n * can reach the integration-tier run that is the evidence gate. What a loop-tier green from this\n * suite means is exactly this:\n *\n *   - PROVED: the decisions in `supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts`,\n *     `supabase/functions/_shared/github.ts` and `supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts`\n *     behave as the nine acceptance criteria this suite lands require. THE THREE MODULES DO NOT\n *     HAVE THE SAME STANDING, and saying they do would be an untrue stated fact: `accounts.ts` and\n *     `github.ts` are imported by the deployed edge functions, byte for byte the code that ships.\n *     `verification.ts` is imported by NO deployed function — it is the module the FUTURE Discovery\n *     send route must import, and today only this suite and\n *     `tests/at/harness/shipped-verification.selftest.ts` import it. That is decision D-D of the\n *     verification leaf's plan and not an oversight; the module's own header says so too.\n *     Every PRODUCT judgement below — every accept and every refusal about account types, the\n *     GitHub precondition, the verified fact and the Discovery floor — comes from those modules,\n *     and so does the onboarding import's content. THE ONE EXCEPTION is the fixture's own\n *     BOOKKEEPING precondition refusals: `sendDiscoveryMessage` and `createOrganization` refuse an\n *     unknown session and an account that never completed signup, which is this storage checking\n *     that the world it was handed exists rather than any shipped rule judging anything. There is\n *     no second copy of the product rules in this file, deliberately: the moment there is one, this\n *     suite is grading a puppet and the green is worth nothing.\n *   - NOT PROVED: that the migration is correct, that either edge function works, that row-level\n *     security denies what it should, that Supabase Auth is configured, or that Google or GitHub\n *     sign-in works. None of that is reachable from here — the storage below is a Map. The evidence\n *     for that half at THIS tree's head is `loop/items/AI4DEV-58/proof-local.txt`, produced against\n *     the live local stack on one machine and EXISTS: 9 checks, 8 passed, 0 failed, 1 skipped. It\n *     covers the migration, the GitHub gate, the imported profile, the database's own backstops\n *     against a caller who never met TypeScript, atomicity, and the provider configuration. THE\n *     SKIP IS THE GITHUB HANDSHAKE: no GitHub OAuth app exists for this project, so \"GitHub\n *     sign-in works\" is not proved by the live tier either. The GOOGLE HANDSHAKE is unproved for\n *     the same reason and was never re-attempted here — no Google credential was in the\n *     environment when the predecessor measured it and none is now. No amount of green anywhere in\n *     this suite proves either handshake. A reviewer cannot reproduce that transcript; it is one\n *     machine's word.\n *\n *     `loop/items/AI4DEV-57/proof-local.txt` is retained for the ONE thing only it still covers:\n *     `create-organization`, which was exercised there and is untouched by this leaf. Its\n *     completion-path and schema evidence is SUPERSEDED — it predates this migration and called a\n *     `complete_signup` that no longer exists.\n *\n * WHAT THE STORAGE HALF DOES MIRROR, because a stand-in that mirrors nothing tests nothing: one\n * account row per auth user (so \"one account holds exactly one global type\" is structural here as it\n * is in the schema, not a rule the adapter remembers), and completion is all-or-nothing (so a body\n * asserting no partial state is asserting the same shape the database's `complete_signup` function\n * guarantees). It mirrors the SHAPE; the database's own guarantee is proved on the live stack.\n *\n * ============================================================================================\n * THE VENDOR MIRRORS, EACH WITH WHAT BINDS IT — the verification leaf's four, named one by one.\n * ============================================================================================\n *\n * A MIRROR IS NOT A PRODUCT JUDGEMENT, and keeping the two apart is what this section is for. The\n * two PRODUCT judgements about verification — whether an address is verified, and whether an\n * unverified caller may send a Discovery message — come from the shipped\n * `supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts` and from nowhere else; there is no second copy of\n * either rule in this file. What is left is a prediction of what Supabase Auth DOES, and a\n * prediction has to say what would prove it wrong.\n *\n *   1. AN EMAIL/PASSWORD REGISTRATION STARTS UNCONFIRMED AND AN EMAIL CARRYING A VERIFICATION\n *      LINK IS SENT.\n *      BOUND — checks (a) and (b) of `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts` on the live stack: the\n *      signup's own wire response, `auth.users.email_confirmed_at IS NULL` read on the database,\n *      and the confirmation email held by the local mail catcher.\n *   2. A LINK THAT WAS NEVER ISSUED CONFIRMS NOTHING.\n *      BOUND — check (b2) of `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts`, and by that check alone. It\n *      follows a TAMPERED variant of the real link — the token mutated, so a token GoTrue never\n *      issued — BEFORE the real link is followed, and reads `auth.users.email_confirmed_at` still\n *      NULL afterwards. This entry used to cite checks (a)-(d), which measure only the POSITIVE\n *      half; two blind reviewers found that overstatement independently, so the check that\n *      measures the negative was added rather than the label softened. Note what this is NOT: it\n *      is not a claim about expiry, single use or resend. The token followed was never minted, so\n *      no lifetime and no use count is in play. AT-001.11 is retired and none of those semantics\n *      is modelled here.\n *   3. USING THE EMAILED LINK SETS `email_confirmed_at`.\n *      BOUND — checks (b) and (d): the link is followed by HTTP GET and the column flips to\n *      non-null, after which the real `/auth/v1/user` response is fed to the SHIPPED\n *      `emailVerifiedFromUser`, which answers true.\n *   4. A PROVIDER REGISTRATION (Google, GitHub) STARTS CONFIRMED, because the provider vouched for\n *      the address.\n *      **UNBOUND, AND SAID PLAINLY.** No OAuth app or credential exists in this environment, so no\n *      live provider session is obtainable — the same recorded gap that leaves the OAuth handshake\n *      itself unproved at every tier, stated in this header since the predecessor item. This item\n *      binds it nowhere and claims nothing about it. NO TEST IN THIS SUITE READS A PROVIDER USER'S\n *      VERIFIED STATE: both verification bodies register by email and password. If the prediction\n *      is wrong — a real provider user arriving unconfirmed — the shipped gate FAILS CLOSED: it\n *      refuses that caller, never allows one. The mirror is bound by the first item that ships a\n *      real provider-path consumer, and not before.\n *\n * THE PROVISIONED PLATFORM ADMINISTRATOR STARTS CONFIRMED, and it is a mirror of a RECIPE rather\n * than of a live measurement. `provisionPlatformAdmin` below marks its auth user confirmed and\n * mints no verification link, because creating an administrator sends no email. The recipe it\n * mirrors is the only one this repository records: the predecessor item's live-proof script creates\n * that user through `POST /auth/v1/admin/users` with `email_confirm: true`. The earlier version of\n * this fixture let the administrator start UNCONFIRMED by reusing the public email path, which\n * contradicted that record.\n * WHAT BINDS IT: nothing yet, and that is said plainly. NOTHING READS AN ADMINISTRATOR'S VERIFIED\n * STATE — not AT-001.07, not anything else in this suite. The predecessor's transcript ran with\n * confirmations OFF, so it does not measure the column under this leaf's flip either. So this\n * mirror is labelled by its recipe and is never called live-bound; the first item that reads an\n * administrator's verified state is the one that binds it.\n *\n * AND THE DISCOVERY SEND SURFACE IS NOT A MIRROR AT ALL — it mirrors nothing, because there is\n * nothing to mirror. No Discovery route exists in this repository at either tier; it is\n * REQ-002/004's. `sendDiscoveryMessage` below is a stand-in surface whose whole job is to put the\n * shipped gate on a tested path. No green over it says anything about enforcement anywhere.\n */\n\nimport type { FixtureWorld, FixtureWorldStore } from '../../harness/fixtures.ts';\nimport {\n  PLATFORM_ACKNOWLEDGMENT_KIND,\n  PUBLIC_SIGNUP_ACCOUNT_TYPES,\n  ngoOnlyActionAllowed,\n  validateCompleteSignup,\n  validateOrganizationName,\n  type AccountType,\n  type CompleteSignupRequest,\n} from '../../../../supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts';\n// BOTH shipped GitHub judgements, not one. The IMPORT SOURCE is the shipped stub, not a copy living\n// in this file — AT-001.05 compares the profile it reads back against `stubGithubStatsFor`, so if the\n// two were separate implementations the test would grade the fixture's copy and say nothing about\n// what the edge function writes. And `extractGithubHandle` is the OTHER shipped decision on this\n// path: `completeSignup` below derives the caller fact through it rather than reading the stored\n// handle straight, exactly as `resolveCaller` does in the edge function. Before that, a regression\n// in the extractor — returning null for a linked identity — would have rejected every linked\n// volunteer at the deployed edge while this suite stayed green.\nimport { extractGithubHandle, stubGithubStatsFor } from '../../../../supabase/functions/_shared/github.ts';\n// BOTH shipped verification judgements, for the same reason both GitHub ones are imported above.\n// `emailVerifiedFromUser` is how the verified fact is DERIVED from the rendered GoTrue user shape\n// on every read — never read straight off storage — and `discoveryMessageAllowed` is the ONLY thing\n// that decides whether a Discovery send is refused. Between them there is no rule about\n// verification left in this file to drift.\nimport {\n  discoveryMessageAllowed,\n  emailVerifiedFromUser,\n} from '../../../../supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts';\nimport type {\n  AccountRow,\n  AccountsSut,\n  AcknowledgmentRow,\n  CompleteSignupOutcome,\n  CreateOrganizationOutcome,\n  MembershipRow,\n  OrganizationRow,\n  SendDiscoveryMessageOutcome,\n  Session,\n  SessionProvider,\n  SignInOutcome,\n  VolunteerProfileRow,\n  World,\n} from './_contract.ts';\n\n/**\n * WHICH REQUIREMENT THIS ADAPTER IS, declared by the adapter itself.\n *\n * `harness/suite-adapters.ts` constrains its map entry to match this literal, and `loadAdapter()` in\n * `harness/index.ts` re-checks it against the requirement it was asked for. Without it the\n * type-check could describe one suite while the run drove another.\n */\nexport const requirement = 'req-001' as const;\n\ninterface AdapterOptions {\n  worlds: FixtureWorldStore;\n}\n\n/** An auth user as Supabase Auth holds one: credentials and the provider that established them. */\ninterface AuthUser {\n  id: string;\n  email: string;\n  password: string | null;\n  provider: SessionProvider;\n  /**\n   * The handle of a LINKED GitHub identity, or null — Auth's `identities[]` narrowed to the one\n   * field anything here reads.\n   *\n   * It is stored on the auth user and NOT on the session, which is the whole reason the gate is a\n   * caller fact: linking happens after a session exists, changes nothing about how that session was\n   * established, and is a property of the user Auth answers for.\n   */\n  githubHandle: string | null;\n  /**\n   * GoTrue's `email_confirmed_at`, or null while the address is unconfirmed — vendor mirror 1 and\n   * 4 in this file's header.\n   *\n   * IT IS STORED AS THE VENDOR'S OWN FIELD VALUE, a nullable instant, and never as a boolean. The\n   * shipped `emailVerifiedFromUser` is what turns it into an answer, and it does so from the\n   * rendered `/auth/v1/user` shape rather than from this field directly. Storing a boolean here\n   * would put the judgement in storage, which is the one thing this file's opening paragraph\n   * promises it does not do.\n   */\n  emailConfirmedAt: string | null;\n  /**\n   * The verification link Auth emailed for this address, or null when no confirmation email was\n   * sent — the fixture's stand-in for the message the local mail catcher holds on the live stack.\n   *\n   * IT IS NOT CLEARED WHEN IT IS USED, deliberately. Clearing it would model SINGLE USE, and\n   * single-use semantics are retired AT-001.11's — \"not stated in REQ-001\". The fixture models\n   * what the criterion states and no more.\n   */\n  verificationLink: string | null;\n}\n\ninterface StoredAcknowledgment extends AcknowledgmentRow {}\n\ninterface State {\n  authUsers: Map<string, AuthUser>;\n  /** email -> auth user id, because sign-in arrives with an address rather than an id */\n  byEmail: Map<string, string>;\n  /**\n   * verification link -> auth user id, because a link arrives on its own with no address beside it\n   * — which is exactly the situation on the live stack, where the link is followed from an inbox.\n   *\n   * A link this map does not hold was never issued, and confirms nothing.\n   */\n  byVerificationLink: Map<string, string>;\n  accounts: Map<string, AccountRow>;\n  organizations: Map<string, OrganizationRow>;\n  /** `${organizationId}:${accountId}` -> row */\n  memberships: Map<string, MembershipRow>;\n  acknowledgments: StoredAcknowledgment[];\n  /** account id -> the imported volunteer profile, mirroring `public.volunteer_profiles`'s primary key */\n  volunteerProfiles: Map<string, VolunteerProfileRow>;\n  /**\n   * account id -> the Discovery message bodies that account has sent.\n   *\n   * There is NO table behind this and no route that writes one; see the header's closing paragraph.\n   * It exists so AT-001.10 can assert that a refused send wrote NOTHING, which the refusal's own\n   * return value cannot show.\n   */\n  discoveryMessages: Map<string, string[]>;\n  nextId: number;\n}\n\nfunction membershipKey(organizationId: string, accountId: string): string {\n  return `${organizationId}:${accountId}`;\n}\n\nfunction clone<T>(value: T): T {\n  return structuredClone(value);\n}\n\nclass AccountsFixtureWorld implements World {\n  constructor(\n    private readonly base: FixtureWorld,\n    private readonly serial: number,\n  ) {}\n\n  email(local: string): string {\n    return `${local}+w${this.serial}@example.test`;\n  }\n\n  async teardown(): Promise<void> {\n    await this.base.teardown();\n  }\n}\n\nexport function createFixtureAdapter({ worlds }: AdapterOptions) {\n  const state: State = {\n    authUsers: new Map(),\n    byEmail: new Map(),\n    byVerificationLink: new Map(),\n    accounts: new Map(),\n    organizations: new Map(),\n    memberships: new Map(),\n    acknowledgments: [],\n    volunteerProfiles: new Map(),\n    discoveryMessages: new Map(),\n    nextId: 1,\n  };\n  const openedWorlds = new Set<AccountsFixtureWorld>();\n  let worldSerial = 0;\n\n  const nextId = (prefix: string): string => `${prefix}-${state.nextId++}`;\n\n  /**\n   * The instant a confirmation is recorded at.\n   *\n   * Fixed rather than read from a clock, for the same reason the acknowledgment's instant is: no\n   * criterion here depends on WHICH instant, only on whether one is present, and a real timestamp\n   * would make the capture non-deterministic for no gain. The shipped `emailVerifiedFromUser`\n   * judges presence and never parses the value, so this is the whole of what the field has to be.\n   */\n  const CONFIRMED_AT = '2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z';\n\n  const register = (\n    email: string,\n    password: string | null,\n    provider: SessionProvider,\n    githubHandle: string | null = null,\n    /**\n     * Whether the CREATOR already confirmed the address, which only an operator can do.\n     *\n     * It exists for `provisionPlatformAdmin` and for nothing else. See the header: the recipe this\n     * repository records creates an administrator through `POST /auth/v1/admin/users` with\n     * `email_confirm: true`, so that user never receives a confirmation email and never has a link.\n     */\n    confirmedByTheCreator = false,\n  ): Session => {\n    // Supabase Auth's own uniqueness, mirrored: a second registration on one address is not a new\n    // user. Left out, a body could accidentally create two auth users for one address and then read\n    // back \"one account per user\" from a situation the real system cannot be in.\n    const existing = state.byEmail.get(email);\n    if (existing) throw new Error(`fixture: ${email} is already registered — Supabase Auth would refuse this`);\n\n    // VENDOR MIRRORS 1 AND 4, and the header names what binds each. A public email/password\n    // registration starts UNCONFIRMED and Auth emails a confirmation link. A registration a\n    // provider vouched for, and one an operator created with the address already confirmed, both\n    // start CONFIRMED and receive no confirmation email, so neither has a link. The provider half\n    // is declared UNBOUND in the header — no OAuth credential exists here — and nothing in this\n    // suite reads a provider user's verified state.\n    const id = nextId('user');\n    const confirmedAtCreation = provider !== 'email' || confirmedByTheCreator;\n    const emailConfirmedAt = confirmedAtCreation ? CONFIRMED_AT : null;\n    // Derived from the user id rather than from a second counter, so the link is unique by\n    // construction and a link read in a failure message says which user it belongs to.\n    const verificationLink = confirmedAtCreation ? null : `verify-${id}`;\n\n    const user: AuthUser = { id, email, password, provider, githubHandle, emailConfirmedAt, verificationLink };\n    state.authUsers.set(user.id, user);\n    state.byEmail.set(email, user.id);\n    if (verificationLink !== null) state.byVerificationLink.set(verificationLink, user.id);\n    return { accountId: user.id, email: user.email, provider: user.provider };\n  };\n\n  /**\n   * The stored auth state rendered as the canonical GoTrue `/auth/v1/user` response shape.\n   *\n   * ONE RENDERER FOR BOTH SHIPPED EXTRACTORS. `extractGithubHandle` reads `identities[]` and\n   * `emailVerifiedFromUser` reads `email_confirmed_at`, and both read them off the SAME object here\n   * — which is the point: on the live stack they read one response body, so rendering two different\n   * shapes for them would be a divergence this suite could never notice.\n   *\n   * WHAT IS STILL NOT PROVED by anything that calls this: that this shape is the shape GoTrue really\n   * sends. That is a prediction of the vendor. The live proof is what binds it.\n   */\n  const renderAuthUser = (user: AuthUser): Record<string, unknown> => ({\n    id: user.id,\n    email: user.email,\n    email_confirmed_at: user.emailConfirmedAt,\n    identities:\n      user.githubHandle === null\n        ? []\n        : [{ provider: 'github', identity_data: { user_name: user.githubHandle } }],\n  });\n\n  /**\n   * Which providers can sign this user back in — Auth's `identities[]`, reduced to what AT-001.02\n   * asks about.\n   *\n   * A GitHub signup and a later GitHub LINK produce the same answer here, deliberately: after either,\n   * the account carries a GitHub identity, and \"a later sign-in via GitHub returns to the same\n   * account\" is a statement about the identity, not about which provider happened to create the user.\n   */\n  const reachableThroughProvider = (user: AuthUser, provider: SessionProvider): boolean =>\n    provider === 'github' ? user.githubHandle !== null : user.provider === provider;\n\n  /**\n   * The `complete-signup` operation, at the loop tier.\n   *\n   * EVERY DECISION ON THIS PATH COMES FROM THE SHIPPED MODULE. What is left here is the four writes,\n   * performed together or not at all — which is the shape `public.complete_signup` guarantees inside\n   * one transaction on the real database, and which step 7 of the plan proves there.\n   */\n  const completeSignup = async (\n    session: Session,\n    request: CompleteSignupRequest,\n    ip: string,\n  ): Promise<CompleteSignupOutcome> => {\n    const authUser = state.authUsers.get(session.accountId);\n    if (!authUser) {\n      return { ok: false, reason: 'no authenticated user — sign in before completing signup' };\n    }\n\n    // THE CALLER FACT COMES FROM THE STORED AUTH USER, never from the request. That is the same\n    // shape the edge function has, where it comes from `/auth/v1/user` — a client cannot assert it\n    // in either place, which is the whole security property of the volunteer gate.\n    //\n    // AND IT IS DERIVED, NOT READ. The stored state is rendered as the canonical GoTrue\n    // `/auth/v1/user` shape — an `identities[]` array, empty when nothing is linked — and the handle\n    // is then judged out of it by the SHIPPED `extractGithubHandle`, which is precisely what\n    // `resolveCaller` does with the real response. Passing `authUser.githubHandle` in directly, as\n    // this used to, pre-narrowed the fact and left the extractor unexecuted by any test: storage\n    // would have been doing a judgement's job, which is the one thing this file's opening paragraph\n    // promises it does not do. What is still NOT proved here is GoTrue's real serialisation — that\n    // this shape is the shape Auth sends. Only the live proof touches that.\n    //\n    // The rendering itself is `renderAuthUser` above, shared with the verified-fact read, because\n    // on the live stack both facts come out of ONE response body and two renderings here could\n    // diverge with nothing able to notice.\n    const callerUser = renderAuthUser(authUser);\n    const decision = validateCompleteSignup(request, { githubHandle: extractGithubHandle(callerUser) });\n    if (!decision.ok) return { ok: false, reason: decision.reason };\n    const { accountType, organizationName, acknowledgmentTextVersion, githubHandle } = decision.value;\n\n    // ONE ROW PER AUTH USER is what makes \"one account holds exactly one global type\" structural\n    // rather than remembered — the schema states it as a primary key, and this states it as a\n    // refusal at the same point.\n    if (state.accounts.has(session.accountId)) {\n      return { ok: false, reason: 'this account has already completed signup — one account holds exactly one global type' };\n    }\n\n    const account: AccountRow = { id: session.accountId, accountType };\n    let organization: OrganizationRow | null = null;\n    let membership: MembershipRow | null = null;\n\n    if (organizationName !== null) {\n      organization = { id: nextId('org'), name: organizationName };\n      membership = { organizationId: organization.id, accountId: account.id, role: 'admin' };\n    }\n\n    // THE ONBOARDING IMPORT, COMPUTED HERE AND WRITTEN BELOW WITH EVERYTHING ELSE — which is the\n    // shape `public.complete_signup` guarantees inside one transaction on the real database. There\n    // is no queue and no deferred job: a volunteer completion either lands the account AND the\n    // populated profile, or lands nothing. AT-001.05's \"a queued-but-empty import fails this test\"\n    // is unrepresentable rather than merely untested.\n    let volunteerProfile: VolunteerProfileRow | null = null;\n    if (githubHandle !== null) {\n      const stats = stubGithubStatsFor(githubHandle);\n      volunteerProfile = {\n        accountId: session.accountId,\n        githubHandle,\n        topLanguages: stats.topLanguages,\n        repositoryCount: stats.repositoryCount,\n        contributionSummary: stats.contributionSummary,\n        // Fixed rather than read from a clock, for the same reason the acknowledgment's instant is:\n        // nothing in these criteria depends on WHICH instant, and the live-stack proof reads the\n        // database's own `now()`.\n        importedAt: '2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z',\n      };\n    }\n\n    const acknowledgment: StoredAcknowledgment = {\n      accountId: account.id,\n      kind: PLATFORM_ACKNOWLEDGMENT_KIND,\n      // Fixed rather than read from a clock: nothing in this leaf's four criteria depends on WHICH\n      // instant it is, only that one is recorded, and a real timestamp would make the capture\n      // non-deterministic for no gain. The live-stack proof reads `now()` from the database.\n      acknowledgedAt: '2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z',\n      ip,\n      textVersion: acknowledgmentTextVersion,\n    };\n\n    // All of them, together. Nothing above this line has mutated `state`.\n    state.accounts.set(account.id, account);\n    if (organization) state.organizations.set(organization.id, organization);\n    if (membership) state.memberships.set(membershipKey(membership.organizationId, membership.accountId), membership);\n    if (volunteerProfile) state.volunteerProfiles.set(volunteerProfile.accountId, volunteerProfile);\n    state.acknowledgments.push(acknowledgment);\n\n    return { ok: true, accountId: account.id, organizationId: organization?.id ?? null };\n  };\n\n  const sut: AccountsSut = {\n    // Read straight off the shipped constant. A literal here would be a second statement of the same\n    // fact, and AT-001.07 would then be asserting what this file says rather than what ships.\n    publicSignupAccountTypes: async () => [...PUBLIC_SIGNUP_ACCOUNT_TYPES],\n\n    registerWithEmailPassword: async (email, password) => register(email, password, 'email'),\n    registerWithProvider: async (provider, email) => register(email, null, provider),\n    // A GitHub signup links the GitHub identity it signed up with — that is what makes it a GitHub\n    // signup rather than a session that merely says 'github'. Both halves are recorded here in one\n    // step because Auth performs them in one round trip.\n    registerWithGithub: async (email, githubHandle) => register(email, null, 'github', githubHandle),\n\n    linkGithubIdentity: async (session, githubHandle) => {\n      const user = state.authUsers.get(session.accountId);\n      // A throw, not a refusal: a body that links an identity onto a user Auth never registered has\n      // a bug in the TEST, and returning a polite outcome would let that bug read as a product\n      // refusal further down.\n      if (!user) throw new Error(`fixture: no auth user ${session.accountId} to link a GitHub identity to`);\n      user.githubHandle = githubHandle;\n    },\n\n    signInWithEmailPassword: async (email, password): Promise<SignInOutcome> => {\n      const userId = state.byEmail.get(email);\n      const user = userId ? state.authUsers.get(userId) : undefined;\n      // One reason for both branches: telling \"no such account\" apart from \"wrong password\" is an\n      // existence oracle, and REQ-001 spends AT-001.21 forbidding exactly that shape elsewhere.\n      if (!user || user.password === null || user.password !== password) {\n        return { ok: false, reason: 'the email or password is incorrect' };\n      }\n      return { ok: true, session: { accountId: user.id, email: user.email, provider: user.provider } };\n    },\n\n    signInWithProvider: async (provider, email): Promise<SignInOutcome> => {\n      const userId = state.byEmail.get(email);\n      const user = userId ? state.authUsers.get(userId) : undefined;\n      // One reason again, for the AT-001.21 reason above: \"no such account\" and \"that provider is\n      // not linked to it\" must not be distinguishable to a caller.\n      if (!user || !reachableThroughProvider(user, provider)) {\n        return { ok: false, reason: `no account of this address is reachable through ${provider}` };\n      }\n      // THE SAME ACCOUNT, which is the clause under test. The id comes from the stored user, so a\n      // second identity minted per sign-in would show up as a different `accountId` rather than\n      // being invisible.\n      return { ok: true, session: { accountId: user.id, email: user.email, provider } };\n    },\n\n    // DERIVED THROUGH THE SHIPPED EXTRACTOR, never read off storage. `user.emailConfirmedAt` is\n    // rendered into the canonical `/auth/v1/user` shape and `emailVerifiedFromUser` judges it —\n    // the same R3 pattern `completeSignup` uses for the GitHub handle, and for the same reason:\n    // the extractor is what the future Discovery route will run, so it has to sit on the tested\n    // path rather than being a function no test ever executes.\n    emailVerified: async (accountId) => {\n      const user = state.authUsers.get(accountId);\n      // A throw, not `false`. `false` is the answer for an unverified user, so returning it here\n      // would make a body that read a user Auth never registered look like a correct negative —\n      // and the whole discriminating pair in AT-001.09 turns on telling those two apart.\n      if (!user) throw new Error(`fixture: no auth user ${accountId} whose verified state could be read`);\n      return emailVerifiedFromUser(renderAuthUser(user));\n    },\n\n    emailedVerificationLink: async (email) => {\n      const userId = state.byEmail.get(email);\n      const user = userId ? state.authUsers.get(userId) : undefined;\n      return user?.verificationLink ?? null;\n    },\n\n    // VENDOR MIRRORS 2 AND 3 — the header names what binds each. Using the link Auth emailed\n    // confirms that address; a link that was never issued flips nothing and says so.\n    //\n    // NOTHING HERE MODELS EXPIRY, SINGLE USE OR RESEND. The link is NOT removed from the map after\n    // it is used, because removing it would be single-use semantics, and AT-001.11 — which is\n    // where those semantics lived — is retired as unstated in REQ-001.\n    useVerificationLink: async (link) => {\n      const userId = state.byVerificationLink.get(link);\n      const user = userId ? state.authUsers.get(userId) : undefined;\n      if (!user) return { ok: false };\n      user.emailConfirmedAt = CONFIRMED_AT;\n      return { ok: true };\n    },\n\n    completeSignup,\n\n    // THE STAND-IN SURFACE FOR A ROUTE THAT DOES NOT EXIST — see the header's closing paragraph\n    // and `AccountsSut`'s fourth kind. Every judgement below is the shipped module's; what is left\n    // here is one refusal about bookkeeping and one write.\n    sendDiscoveryMessage: async (session, body): Promise<SendDiscoveryMessageOutcome> => {\n      const authUser = state.authUsers.get(session.accountId);\n      if (!authUser) return { ok: false, reason: 'no authenticated user — sign in before sending a Discovery message' };\n      // The same bookkeeping refusal `createOrganization` gives, and it is THIS FILE'S, not a\n      // product rule: an account that has not completed signup has no sender to record against.\n      // It is checked FIRST so that AT-001.10's refusal is unambiguously the gate's own — the\n      // account there has completed signup, so this branch cannot be what answers.\n      if (!state.accounts.has(session.accountId)) {\n        return { ok: false, reason: 'complete signup before sending a Discovery message' };\n      }\n\n      // THE VERIFIED FACT IS A CALLER FACT, derived exactly as `emailVerified` derives it: from the\n      // rendered response shape, through the shipped extractor. It is never a request field —\n      // a request field is something a client asserts about itself, and a floor built on one is no\n      // floor at all.\n      const emailVerified = emailVerifiedFromUser(renderAuthUser(authUser));\n\n      // THE ONLY DECISION ON THIS PATH, and it is the shipped one. The refusal text is the GATE's,\n      // carried through unchanged — AT-001.10 matches the reason, so restating it here would make\n      // the test grade this file instead of the module that ships.\n      const allowed = discoveryMessageAllowed({ emailVerified });\n      if (!allowed.ok) return { ok: false, reason: allowed.reason };\n\n      // ONLY REACHED ON ALLOW, so a refusal writes nothing — which is what `discoveryMessagesBy`\n      // is read for. The body is stored opaquely: recipients, threads and message state are\n      // REQ-002/004's semantics and none of them is invented here.\n      const sent = state.discoveryMessages.get(session.accountId) ?? [];\n      sent.push(body);\n      state.discoveryMessages.set(session.accountId, sent);\n      return { ok: true };\n    },\n\n    discoveryMessagesBy: async (accountId) => clone(state.discoveryMessages.get(accountId) ?? []),\n\n    createOrganization: async (session, organizationName): Promise<CreateOrganizationOutcome> => {\n      const account = state.accounts.get(session.accountId);\n      if (!account) return { ok: false, reason: 'complete signup before creating an organisation' };\n\n      // THE REFUSAL IS THE SHIPPED MODULE'S, not this file's. That is what makes AT-001.06 a test of\n      // an application boundary rather than of a helper called directly from a test body.\n      const allowed = ngoOnlyActionAllowed(account.accountType);\n      if (!allowed.ok) return { ok: false, reason: allowed.reason };\n\n      // The name rule is the shipped module's too. It used to be spelled out here, which made this\n      // file hold a second copy of a rule — the one thing its opening paragraph promises it does\n      // not do.\n      const name = validateOrganizationName(organizationName);\n      if (!name.ok) return { ok: false, reason: name.reason };\n\n      const organization: OrganizationRow = { id: nextId('org'), name: name.value };\n      const membership: MembershipRow = { organizationId: organization.id, accountId: account.id, role: 'admin' };\n      state.organizations.set(organization.id, organization);\n      state.memberships.set(membershipKey(organization.id, account.id), membership);\n      return { ok: true, organizationId: organization.id };\n    },\n\n    account: async (accountId) => clone(state.accounts.get(accountId) ?? null),\n    organization: async (organizationId) => clone(state.organizations.get(organizationId) ?? null),\n    membership: async (organizationId, accountId) => clone(state.memberships.get(membershipKey(organizationId, accountId)) ?? null),\n    acknowledgments: async (accountId) => clone(state.acknowledgments.filter((row) => row.accountId === accountId)),\n    volunteerProfile: async (accountId) => clone(state.volunteerProfiles.get(accountId) ?? null),\n\n    // The two searches a refused action needs: it hands back no identifier, so the rows it must NOT\n    // have written can only be looked for by the name that was attempted and by who holds what.\n    organizationsNamed: async (name) => clone([...state.organizations.values()].filter((row) => row.name === name.trim())),\n    membershipsOf: async (accountId) => clone([...state.memberships.values()].filter((row) => row.accountId === accountId)),\n\n    // DISCRIMINATING — and WHICH predicate that green grades is worth being exact about, because\n    // this one is not the shipped one. `public.has_platform_acknowledgment` is SQL, and a\n    // TypeScript module cannot supply a SQL predicate; the one judgement inside it — which\n    // acknowledgment kind counts — is shared, which is why the constant below is imported rather\n    // than spelled. So AT-001.01's loop-tier green proves THE RULE and THIS storage: a completion\n    // writes the row, and the answer is false before and true after. The shipped SQL predicate\n    // could still return true unconditionally and this would stay green. What proves that one is\n    // step 7(h) of the plan, against the live database, and nothing else in this item.\n    hasPlatformAcknowledgment: async (accountId) =>\n      state.acknowledgments.some((row) => row.accountId === accountId && row.kind === PLATFORM_ACKNOWLEDGMENT_KIND),\n\n    provisionPlatformAdmin: async (email, password) => {\n      // CONFIRMED AT PROVISIONING, AND NO VERIFICATION LINK — the header's administrator paragraph\n      // says why: the only provisioning recipe this repository records creates the user with\n      // `email_confirm: true`, and creating an administrator sends no email.\n      const session = register(email, password, 'email', null, true);\n      // Written directly, bypassing `completeSignup` — which is not a shortcut but the point:\n      // `parseAccountType` refuses this type, so the public path CANNOT produce it, and the only way\n      // an administrator exists is an authority the public never holds. The type is still taken from\n      // the shipped vocabulary rather than spelled as a literal.\n      const accountType: AccountType = 'platform_admin';\n      state.accounts.set(session.accountId, { id: session.accountId, accountType });\n      return session;\n    },\n  };\n\n  return {\n    sut: { accounts: sut },\n    fixtures: {\n      world: async (name: string) => {\n        const base = await worlds.world(name);\n        const world = new AccountsFixtureWorld(base, ++worldSerial);\n        openedWorlds.add(world);\n        return world;\n      },\n    },\n    teardown: async () => {\n      await Promise.all([...openedWorlds].map((world) => world.teardown()));\n      openedWorlds.clear();\n      state.authUsers.clear();\n      state.byEmail.clear();\n      state.byVerificationLink.clear();\n      state.accounts.clear();\n      state.organizations.clear();\n      state.memberships.clear();\n      state.acknowledgments.length = 0;\n      state.volunteerProfiles.clear();\n      state.discoveryMessages.clear();\n      state.nextId = 1;\n    },\n  };\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":661,"totalLines":661,"truncated":false}},"title":"tests\\at\\suites\\req-001\\_fixture.ts","time":{"start":1786290434558,"end":1786290434571}},"id":"prt_fe734f0ed001AMHn7PtydLkLnQ","sessionID":"ses_018cbdef8ffeIiDUqdR6dh2IlP","messageID":"msg_fe734da68001vMIP14G4j0Ngat"}}
0c389c633b9867b93b1466a77c75d5bc9df66f56:loop/items/AI4DEV-59/artifacts/audit-luna.stderr.log:10252:C:\Users\nirdr\Downloads\ai4good\.claude\worktrees\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\loop\items\AI4DEV-59\artifacts\audit-flash.events.jsonl:33:{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1786290444182,"sessionID":"ses_018cbdef8ffeIiDUqdR6dh2IlP","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_00_wof8OsVw4YgOgoFgxgmI3660","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\tests\\at\\suites\\req-001\\_contract.ts"},"output":"<path>C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\tests\\at\\suites\\req-001\\_contract.ts</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: /**\n2:  * What REQ-001 adds to the shared harness contract.\n3:  *\n4:  * The generic seams — tier, clock, fixture worlds, sentinels, faults, static scan, config, vendor\n5:  * sims, the harness shape itself — live in `tests/at/harness/contracts.ts` and are re-exported here\n6:  * so a test body imports one file. What is genuinely REQ-001's is below: the accounts system under\n7:  * test, the row shapes its assertions read back, and what this requirement's fixture world can do.\n8:  *\n9:  * TYPE ALIASES, NOT INTERFACES, throughout — the rule `harness/contracts.ts` states and gives its\n10:  * reasons for. Nothing reachable from the objects `open()` hands a test body may be an interface,\n11:  * because an interface can be reopened with `declare module` and an OPTIONAL member merged into it\n12:  * reads `undefined` at run time while every type-check stays green.\n13:  *\n14:  * THE JUDGEMENT TYPES ARE IMPORTED FROM THE SHIPPED MODULE, not restated here. `AccountType`,\n15:  * `CompleteSignupRequest` and the rest come from `supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts`, which is\n16:  * the same module the two edge functions import. Restating them would be two independent statements\n17:  * about one thing with nothing able to notice them diverging — and that is the defect this whole\n18:  * harness exists to delete, not a shape to reproduce inside it.\n19:  */\n20: \n21: import type { WorldSeam } from '../../harness/contracts.ts';\n22: import type {\n23:   AccountType,\n24:   CompleteSignupRequest,\n25: } from '../../../../supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts';\n26: \n27: export type {\n28:   Clock,\n29:   ConfigRegistry,\n30:   FaultHandle,\n31:   Faults,\n32:   Fixtures,\n33:   ProviderOutcome,\n34:   Sentinel,\n35:   Sentinels,\n36:   StaticScan,\n37:   Tier,\n38:   WorldSeam,\n39: } from '../../harness/contracts.ts';\n40: export { TIERS } from '../../harness/contracts.ts';\n41: \n42: export type { AccountType, CompleteSignupRequest };\n43: \n44: /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------- what gets read back */\n45: \n46: /** One row of `public.accounts` — one per authenticated user, holding exactly one global type. */\n47: export type AccountRow = {\n48:   id: string;\n49:   accountType: AccountType;\n50: };\n51: \n52: /** One row of `public.organizations`. */\n53: export type OrganizationRow = {\n54:   id: string;\n55:   name: string;\n56: };\n57: \n58: /** One row of `public.org_memberships` — the per-NGO role, which is NOT the global account type. */\n59: export type MembershipRow = {\n60:   organizationId: string;\n61:   accountId: string;\n62:   role: 'admin' | 'member';\n63: };\n64: \n65: /**\n66:  * One row of `public.acknowledgments` — EXACTLY the three fields AT-001.01 names recorded, plus the\n67:  * account and kind that identify it.\n68:  *\n69:  * Name, title and authority attestation are AT-001.19's fields and belong to the acknowledgment-\n70:  * identity deliverable (`loop/decomp/req-001.md` D4.L1), which is declared red by this leaf. They\n71:  * are deliberately absent here: adding columns for a criterion this leaf does not test would be\n72:  * speculation, and a reader would have no way to tell it from work that had been done.\n73:  */\n74: export type AcknowledgmentRow = {\n75:   accountId: string;\n76:   kind: string;\n77:   /** ISO-8601 instant — AT-001.01's \"timestamp\" */\n78:   acknowledgedAt: string;\n79:   /**\n80:    * AT-001.01's \"IP\": the address the gateway chain REPORTED, never a verified source address.\n81:    * Measured on the live local stack — a spoofed `x-forwarded-for` is stored verbatim, and with no\n82:    * header at all the stored value is the gateway's own hop. See `callerIp` in\n83:    * `supabase/functions/_shared/edge.ts`.\n84:    */\n85:   ip: string;\n86:   /** which version of the ToS + Platform Promise text was accepted — AT-001.01's \"text version\" */\n87:   textVersion: string;\n88: };\n89: \n90: /**\n91:  * One row of `public.volunteer_profiles` — AT-001.05's \"profile\", and every column of it.\n92:  *\n93:  * THE STATS ARE THE STUB IMPORT FIXTURE'S, NOT GITHUB'S. `stubGithubStatsFor` in\n94:  * `supabase/functions/_shared/github.ts` produces them, the decomposition manifest's cross-contract\n95:  * ratifies that stand-in (\"stub import fixture until W3\"), and no code in this item calls\n96:  * api.github.com. What the row proves is that onboarding FIRED and landed POPULATED — the\n97:  * criterion's \"a queued-but-empty import fails this test\" — and not that any statistic is real.\n98:  */\n99: export type VolunteerProfileRow = {\n100:   accountId: string;\n101:   /** the handle of the GitHub identity linked to the account — AT-001.05's \"linked handle\" */\n102:   githubHandle: string;\n103:   /** AT-001.05's \"top languages\"; never empty, in the schema by CHECK and here by assertion */\n104:   topLanguages: string[];\n105:   /** AT-001.05's \"repository count\" */\n106:   repositoryCount: number;\n107:   /** AT-001.05's \"contribution summary\" */\n108:   contributionSummary: string;\n109:   /** ISO-8601 instant the import landed */\n110:   importedAt: string;\n111: };\n112: \n113: /* --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- the session */\n114: \n115: /**\n116:  * An authenticated session, and WHICH PROVIDER ESTABLISHED IT.\n117:  *\n118:  * `provider` is recorded, never simulated. Supabase Auth records the provider that produced a\n119:  * session; how the session was obtained — an email and a password, or a completed Google consent\n120:  * round trip — happens entirely upstream of anything this requirement's leaf ships. AT-001.03's\n121:  * body asserts that `completeSignup` behaves identically for `'google'` and `'email'`, which is a\n122:  * real property of the shipped decision module (it never reads this field). It is NOT a claim that a\n123:  * Google round trip works: see the suite's own note on AT-001.03, and the plan's per-id table.\n124:  *\n125:  * `'github'` joins the two for AT-001.02 and carries exactly the same caveat: a github-established\n126:  * session is the state Auth is in AFTER a consent round trip, and no handshake is simulated to reach\n127:  * it. The establishing provider still participates in NO decision the shipped modules make — the\n128:  * volunteer gate reads a LINKED IDENTITY, which is a different fact (see `linkGithubIdentity`).\n129:  */\n130: export type SessionProvider = 'email' | 'google' | 'github';\n131: \n132: export type Session = {\n133:   /** the auth user id, which is also the `public.accounts` primary key once signup completes */\n134:   accountId: string;\n135:   email: string;\n136:   provider: SessionProvider;\n137: };\n138: \n139: /* -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- the outcomes */\n140: \n141: export type SignInOutcome = { ok: true; session: Session } | { ok: false; reason: string };\n142: \n143: export type CompleteSignupOutcome =\n144:   | { ok: true; accountId: string; organizationId: string | null }\n145:   | { ok: false; reason: string };\n146: \n147: export type CreateOrganizationOutcome = { ok: true; organizationId: string } | { ok: false; reason: string };\n148: \n149: /**\n150:  * The outcome of an attempted Discovery message — and the refusal carries WHY, for the reason\n151:  * `Decision` in the shipped module gives: AT-001.10 asserts that the block NAMES verification as\n152:  * the remedy, so a bare boolean would make the criterion untestable.\n153:  */\n154: export type SendDiscoveryMessageOutcome = { ok: true } | { ok: false; reason: string };\n155: \n156: /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ the SUT */\n157: \n158: /**\n159:  * REQ-001's accounts system under test.\n160:  *\n161:  * The split is the point, and there are now FOUR kinds of member here rather than three:\n162:  *\n163:  *   1. THE TWO PRODUCT OPERATIONS — `completeSignup` and `createOrganization`. At loop tier the\n164:  *      adapter runs them over its own storage but delegates every judgement to\n165:  *      `supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts`, the module the edge functions import; at\n166:  *      integration tier they would be the deployed edge functions.\n167:  *   2. SUPABASE AUTH'S HALF — registration, sign-in, identity linking, and the verification state\n168:  *      (`emailVerified`, `emailedVerificationLink`, `useVerificationLink`). None of it is this\n169:  *      requirement's code. It is what produces the states the product operations judge.\n170:  *   3. READ-BACK — the row shapes an assertion reads after the fact.\n171:  *   4. ONE STAND-IN SURFACE, AND IT IS THE ONLY ONE — `sendDiscoveryMessage`, with\n172:  *      `discoveryMessagesBy` reading it back.\n173:  *\n174:  * THE FOURTH KIND NEEDS ITS OWN SENTENCE, because it is the one that could be misread. NO\n175:  * DISCOVERY ROUTE EXISTS IN THIS TREE. The Discovery message route is REQ-002/004's, and what\n176:  * this requirement's leaf ships is the DECISION that route must consult —\n177:  * `discoveryMessageAllowed` in `supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts`. So\n178:  * `sendDiscoveryMessage` is not a deployed operation being tested; it is a stand-in surface whose\n179:  * only job is to put the shipped decision on a tested path, exactly as\n180:  * `hasPlatformAcknowledgment` below is the hook a project-creation leaf must call. A green over\n181:  * it says the DECISION is right. It says nothing about enforcement anywhere, because there is\n182:  * nowhere yet to enforce it.\n183:  */\n184: export type AccountsSut = {\n185:   /** AT-001.07's second clause: which types the PUBLIC signup surface offers. */\n186:   publicSignupAccountTypes(): Promise<readonly string[]>;\n187: \n188:   /* --- Supabase Auth's half. Not this leaf's code; it is what produces a session to complete. --- */\n189: \n190:   /**\n191:    * Register an auth user with an email and a password, and return the resulting session.\n192:    *\n193:    * IT STILL RETURNS A SESSION, AND THE HONEST GAP THAT LEAVES IS STATED HERE RATHER THAN HIDDEN.\n194:    * With `enable_confirmations = true` the live GoTrue issues NO session at signup, so on the real\n195:    * stack a completed-but-unverified account is not reachable by the public path. This fixture's\n196:    * `Session` has always been an identity handle rather than an access token, and session ISSUANCE\n197:    * is D2.L2's subject (AT-001.12, .13 — declared red). So at the loop tier such an account IS\n198:    * constructible, and AT-001.10's body constructs one deliberately: the gate exists because\n199:    * decision-8 makes verification the write path's own floor, not a property borrowed from the\n200:    * session layer. The live-stack behaviour is measured in `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts`\n201:    * checks (a) and (c), and nowhere else in this item.\n202:    */\n203:   registerWithEmailPassword(email: string, password: string): Promise<Session>;\n204:   /**\n205:    * A session Auth recorded as having come from an external provider.\n206:    *\n207:    * It performs NO handshake and fabricates no authorization code or token exchange: it is the state\n208:    * Auth is in AFTER a consent round trip, which is the only part of the story any agent can reach.\n209:    */\n210:   registerWithProvider(provider: SessionProvider, email: string): Promise<Session>;\n211:   /**\n212:    * A GitHub OAuth signup, as Auth records it: a session established by GitHub whose account already\n213:    * carries the linked GitHub identity — AT-001.02's \"signs up via GitHub OAuth\".\n214:    *\n215:    * Like `registerWithProvider` it performs NO handshake. It is `registerWithProvider('github', …)`\n216:    * plus the fact that a GitHub signup necessarily links the GitHub identity it signed up with, and\n217:    * it is a separate member rather than an argument because that second half is what AT-001.02's\n218:    * \"the GitHub identity is linked to it\" clause is about.\n219:    */\n220:   registerWithGithub(email: string, githubHandle: string): Promise<Session>;\n221:   /**\n222:    * The state Auth is in after a `linkIdentity` round trip — AT-001.04's \"linking completes signup\".\n223:    *\n224:    * A LINKED IDENTITY IS NOT THE ESTABLISHING PROVIDER, and keeping the two apart is the point of\n225:    * this member existing at all: an email- or Google-established volunteer links GitHub and their\n226:    * session's `provider` does not change. The gate in\n227:    * `supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts` reads the link; nothing shipped reads the provider.\n228:    *\n229:    * Same posture as every other Auth member here — no handshake, no fabricated authorization code.\n230:    */\n231:   linkGithubIdentity(session: Session, githubHandle: string): Promise<void>;\n232:   /** Return sign-in with the same credentials — AT-001.01's final clause. */\n233:   signInWithEmailPassword(email: string, password: string): Promise<SignInOutcome>;\n234:   /**\n235:    * The return visit through an external provider — AT-001.02's \"a later sign-in via GitHub returns\n236:    * to the same account\".\n237:    *\n238:    * What it asserts is IDENTITY CONTINUITY, which is the half of that clause reachable without a\n239:    * person: an existing user whose account carries that provider's identity signs in to the SAME\n240:    * account rather than to a second one. The consent round trip itself is not performed and is named\n241:    * unproved in the plan's per-id table.\n242:    */\n243:   signInWithProvider(provider: SessionProvider, email: string): Promise<SignInOutcome>;\n244: \n245:   /* ------------------------- Supabase Auth's verification state ------------------------------- */\n246: \n247:   /**\n248:    * Whether Supabase Auth reports this account's email address confirmed — AT-001.09's observable.\n249:    *\n250:    * IT IS DERIVED, NOT STORED-AND-READ. The adapter renders the account's stored Auth state as the\n251:    * canonical GoTrue `/auth/v1/user` shape and judges the answer out of it with the SHIPPED\n252:    * `emailVerifiedFromUser`. That is the same pattern `completeSignup` uses for the GitHub handle\n253:    * (the GitHub leaf's gate-2 ruling R3), and it exists so the shipped extractor sits on the tested\n254:    * path rather than being a function no test ever runs — which is exactly how the future Discovery\n255:    * route will resolve the same fact from the same response.\n256:    *\n257:    * WHAT NO LOOP-TIER GREEN OVER IT PROVES: that the rendered shape is the shape GoTrue really\n258:    * sends. Only `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts` check (d) touches that, by feeding a real\n259:    * response to the same shipped function.\n260:    */\n261:   emailVerified(accountId: string): Promise<boolean>;\n262:   /**\n263:    * The verification link emailed to this address, or `null` when none was emailed.\n264:    *\n265:    * `null` is a real answer rather than only the empty case: a provider registration is confirmed\n266:    * by the provider and no confirmation email is sent for it.\n267:    */\n268:   emailedVerificationLink(email: string): Promise<string | null>;\n269:   /**\n270:    * Use a verification link. Using the link Auth emailed for an address confirms that address; a\n271:    * link that was never issued returns `ok: false` and flips nothing.\n272:    *\n273:    * NO EXPIRY, NO SINGLE USE AND NO RESEND IS MODELLED OR ASSERTED. AT-001.11 is retired — the\n274:    * acceptance file's own words are that \"verification-link expiry/single-use/resend semantics are\n275:    * not stated in REQ-001\" — so building any of them here would be asserting a criterion that was\n276:    * deliberately withdrawn.\n277:    *\n278:    * THE NEVER-ISSUED NEGATIVE IS NOT AT-001.11 GROUND, and the difference is worth being exact\n279:    * about. It is not a claim about a link's lifetime; it guards this test's own oracle. A\n280:    * link-shaped string that verified an account it never belonged to would make \"using the emailed\n281:    * link is what flips it\" mean nothing at all, because any string would flip anything.\n282:    */\n283:   useVerificationLink(link: string): Promise<{ ok: boolean }>;\n284: \n285:   /* ----------------------------------- the two product operations ----------------------------- */\n286: \n287:   /** `supabase/functions/complete-signup` — turns an authenticated auth user into a typed account. */\n288:   completeSignup(session: Session, request: CompleteSignupRequest, ip: string): Promise<CompleteSignupOutcome>;\n289:   /** `supabase/functions/create-organization` — the NGO-only action AT-001.06 drives. */\n290:   createOrganization(session: Session, organizationName: string): Promise<CreateOrganizationOutcome>;\n291: \n292:   /* --------------------------- the Discovery gate's stand-in surface -------------------------- */\n293: \n294:   /**\n295:    * Attempt to send a Discovery message — AT-001.10's action.\n296:    *\n297:    * THERE IS NO DEPLOYED ROUTE BEHIND THIS, at either tier. See the fourth kind in this type's own\n298:    * header: the Discovery route is REQ-002/004's and does not exist, so this member is a stand-in\n299:    * surface that exists to put the SHIPPED `discoveryMessageAllowed` on a tested path. At loop\n300:    * tier the adapter derives the caller's verified fact through the shipped\n301:    * `emailVerifiedFromUser`, consults the shipped gate, and refuses with the GATE's own reason.\n302:    *\n303:    * THE BODY IS AN OPAQUE STRING AND NOTHING ELSE. Recipients, threads, attachments and message\n304:    * state are REQ-002/004's semantics; inventing any of them here to make this member look real\n305:    * would be building another requirement's surface early. What AT-001.10 needs is an attempted\n306:    * write and a way to see whether it happened, and one string supplies both.\n307:    */\n308:   sendDiscoveryMessage(session: Session, body: string): Promise<SendDiscoveryMessageOutcome>;\n309:   /**\n310:    * The Discovery message bodies this account has sent — read-back, and it is not optional.\n311:    *\n312:    * A BLOCK WHOSE WRITE HAPPENED ANYWAY IS NOT A BLOCK. The refusal's own return value cannot show\n313:    * that nothing was written, for the same reason `organizationsNamed` exists: a refusal hands\n314:    * back no identifier, so the row that must NOT be there can only be looked for by who would have\n315:    * written it.\n316:    */\n317:   discoveryMessagesBy(accountId: string): Promise<string[]>;\n318: \n319:   /* ------------------------------------------- read-back -------------------------------------- */\n320: \n321:   account(accountId: string): Promise<AccountRow | null>;\n322:   organization(organizationId: string): Promise<OrganizationRow | null>;\n323:   membership(organizationId: string, accountId: string): Promise<MembershipRow | null>;\n324:   acknowledgments(accountId: string): Promise<AcknowledgmentRow[]>;\n325:   /**\n326:    * The volunteer's imported GitHub profile, or `null` — AT-001.05's observable.\n327:    *\n328:    * `null` is a real and asserted answer, not merely the empty case: AT-001.05's pre-completion\n329:    * negative reads it AFTER the identity is linked and BEFORE the signup completes, and requires\n330:    * null there. That is what proves the population is CAUSED by completion rather than sitting in a\n331:    * queue somewhere waiting to be drained.\n332:    */\n333:   volunteerProfile(accountId: string): Promise<VolunteerProfileRow | null>;\n334: \n335:   /**\n336:    * The two read-backs a REFUSED action needs, which the keyed ones above cannot serve.\n337:    *\n338:    * Asserting that a rejection wrote nothing means looking for rows whose identifiers were never\n339:    * handed out — a refused `createOrganization` returns no organisation id. So one search by the\n340:    * name that was attempted, and one listing of everything an account is a member of. Without them\n341:    * the strongest statement available is \"the call said no\", and an implementation that writes both\n342:    * rows and then says no would satisfy it.\n343:    */\n344:   organizationsNamed(name: string): Promise<OrganizationRow[]>;\n345:   membershipsOf(accountId: string): Promise<MembershipRow[]>;\n346:   /**\n347:    * `public.has_platform_acknowledgment(account_id)` — the observable form of AT-001.01's \"before\n348:    * any project creation is possible\".\n349:    *\n350:    * It MUST DISCRIMINATE: false for an authenticated user who has not completed signup, true after.\n351:    * The body asserts both halves for exactly that reason.\n352:    *\n353:    * WHICH IMPLEMENTATION THAT ASSERTION REACHES depends on the tier, and the difference matters. At\n354:    * loop tier it reaches the fixture adapter's storage query, so what goes green is the RULE and the\n355:    * adapter's storage — the shipped SQL function could return true unconditionally and the loop tier\n356:    * would not notice. `public.has_platform_acknowledgment` itself is proved by step 7(h) of the\n357:    * plan, against the live database, and by nothing else in this item.\n358:    *\n359:    * What this leaf cannot do at either tier is ENFORCE the clause — nothing in the tree creates a\n360:    * project, and building project creation belongs to another requirement. This is the hook the leaf\n361:    * that lands project creation must call.\n362:    */\n363:   hasPlatformAcknowledgment(accountId: string): Promise<boolean>;\n364: \n365:   /* ------------------------------------ the provisioned admin --------------------------------- */\n366: \n367:   /**\n368:    * Provision a `platform_admin` — the ONLY legal way one exists, because the public path refuses\n369:    * the type. It sits apart from `completeSignup` rather than being one of its options for that\n370:    * reason.\n371:    *\n372:    * ON THE LIVE STACK THIS IS **NOT** A SERVICE-ROLE WRITE, and the correction matters more than\n373:    * most: this comment used to say it was, and a later reader following it would have granted the\n374:    * service role an INSERT on `public.accounts` — a write path straight past `complete_signup`'s\n375:    * `platform_admin` refusal, which is the one guard that sits on the only write path. The service\n376:    * role holds no INSERT anywhere in this schema, by measurement and by decision. Provisioning is a\n377:    * direct database operation by an operator: a NARROWER authority than the service role, not a\n378:    * wider one, and not one any running service holds.\n379:    */\n380:   provisionPlatformAdmin(email: string, password: string): Promise<Session>;\n381: };\n382: \n383: /* -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- the world */\n384: \n385: /**\n386:  * What REQ-001's fixture world adds to the shared seam.\n387:  *\n388:  * An INTERSECTION, not `interface World extends WorldSeam`, for the alias reason above.\n389:  *\n390:  * One member, and it earns its place: every test in this suite registers auth users, and two tests\n391:  * that happened to pick the same address would interfere in a way that looks like a product defect.\n392:  * `email()` namespaces an address to the world that issued it, so isolation is structural rather\n393:  * than a convention each body has to remember.\n394:  */\n395: export type World = WorldSeam & {\n396:   /** a mail address unique to THIS world, e.g. `email('ngo')` */\n397:   email(local: string): string;\n398: };\n\n(End of file - total 398 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"/**\n * What REQ-001 adds to the shared harness contract.\n *\n * The generic seams — tier, clock, fixture worlds, sentinels, faults, static scan, config, vendor\n * sims, the harness shape itself — live in `tests/at/harness/contracts.ts` and are re-exported here\n * so a test body imports one file. What is genuinely REQ-001's is below: the accounts system under\n * test, the row shapes its assertions read back, and what this requirement's fixture world can do.\n *\n * TYPE ALIASES, NOT INTERFACES, throughout — the rule `harness/contracts.ts` states and gives its\n * reasons for. Nothing reachable from the objects `open()` hands a test body may be an interface,\n * because an interface can be reopened with `declare module` and an OPTIONAL member merged into it\n * reads `undefined` at run time while every type-check stays green.\n *\n * THE JUDGEMENT TYPES ARE IMPORTED FROM THE SHIPPED MODULE, not restated here. `AccountType`,\n * `CompleteSignupRequest` and the rest come from `supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts`, which is\n * the same module the two edge functions import. Restating them would be two independent statements\n * about one thing with nothing able to notice them diverging — and that is the defect this whole\n * harness exists to delete, not a shape to reproduce inside it.\n */\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-aa95110cbeab31aa7\\tests\\at\\suites\\req-001\\_contract.ts","text":"/**\n * What REQ-001 adds to the shared harness contract.\n *\n * The generic seams — tier, clock, fixture worlds, sentinels, faults, static scan, config, vendor\n * sims, the harness shape itself — live in `tests/at/harness/contracts.ts` and are re-exported here\n * so a test body imports one file. What is genuinely REQ-001's is below: the accounts system under\n * test, the row shapes its assertions read back, and what this requirement's fixture world can do.\n *\n * TYPE ALIASES, NOT INTERFACES, throughout — the rule `harness/contracts.ts` states and gives its\n * reasons for. Nothing reachable from the objects `open()` hands a test body may be an interface,\n * because an interface can be reopened with `declare module` and an OPTIONAL member merged into it\n * reads `undefined` at run time while every type-check stays green.\n *\n * THE JUDGEMENT TYPES ARE IMPORTED FROM THE SHIPPED MODULE, not restated here. `AccountType`,\n * `CompleteSignupRequest` and the rest come from `supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts`, which is\n * the same module the two edge functions import. Restating them would be two independent statements\n * about one thing with nothing able to notice them diverging — and that is the defect this whole\n * harness exists to delete, not a shape to reproduce inside it.\n */\n\nimport type { WorldSeam } from '../../harness/contracts.ts';\nimport type {\n  AccountType,\n  CompleteSignupRequest,\n} from '../../../../supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts';\n\nexport type {\n  Clock,\n  ConfigRegistry,\n  FaultHandle,\n  Faults,\n  Fixtures,\n  ProviderOutcome,\n  Sentinel,\n  Sentinels,\n  StaticScan,\n  Tier,\n  WorldSeam,\n} from '../../harness/contracts.ts';\nexport { TIERS } from '../../harness/contracts.ts';\n\nexport type { AccountType, CompleteSignupRequest };\n\n/* ------------------------------------------------------------------------- what gets read back */\n\n/** One row of `public.accounts` — one per authenticated user, holding exactly one global type. */\nexport type AccountRow = {\n  id: string;\n  accountType: AccountType;\n};\n\n/** One row of `public.organizations`. */\nexport type OrganizationRow = {\n  id: string;\n  name: string;\n};\n\n/** One row of `public.org_memberships` — the per-NGO role, which is NOT the global account type. */\nexport type MembershipRow = {\n  organizationId: string;\n  accountId: string;\n  role: 'admin' | 'member';\n};\n\n/**\n * One row of `public.acknowledgments` — EXACTLY the three fields AT-001.01 names recorded, plus the\n * account and kind that identify it.\n *\n * Name, title and authority attestation are AT-001.19's fields and belong to the acknowledgment-\n * identity deliverable (`loop/decomp/req-001.md` D4.L1), which is declared red by this leaf. They\n * are deliberately absent here: adding columns for a criterion this leaf does not test would be\n * speculation, and a reader would have no way to tell it from work that had been done.\n */\nexport type AcknowledgmentRow = {\n  accountId: string;\n  kind: string;\n  /** ISO-8601 instant — AT-001.01's \"timestamp\" */\n  acknowledgedAt: string;\n  /**\n   * AT-001.01's \"IP\": the address the gateway chain REPORTED, never a verified source address.\n   * Measured on the live local stack — a spoofed `x-forwarded-for` is stored verbatim, and with no\n   * header at all the stored value is the gateway's own hop. See `callerIp` in\n   * `supabase/functions/_shared/edge.ts`.\n   */\n  ip: string;\n  /** which version of the ToS + Platform Promise text was accepted — AT-001.01's \"text version\" */\n  textVersion: string;\n};\n\n/**\n * One row of `public.volunteer_profiles` — AT-001.05's \"profile\", and every column of it.\n *\n * THE STATS ARE THE STUB IMPORT FIXTURE'S, NOT GITHUB'S. `stubGithubStatsFor` in\n * `supabase/functions/_shared/github.ts` produces them, the decomposition manifest's cross-contract\n * ratifies that stand-in (\"stub import fixture until W3\"), and no code in this item calls\n * api.github.com. What the row proves is that onboarding FIRED and landed POPULATED — the\n * criterion's \"a queued-but-empty import fails this test\" — and not that any statistic is real.\n */\nexport type VolunteerProfileRow = {\n  accountId: string;\n  /** the handle of the GitHub identity linked to the account — AT-001.05's \"linked handle\" */\n  githubHandle: string;\n  /** AT-001.05's \"top languages\"; never empty, in the schema by CHECK and here by assertion */\n  topLanguages: string[];\n  /** AT-001.05's \"repository count\" */\n  repositoryCount: number;\n  /** AT-001.05's \"contribution summary\" */\n  contributionSummary: string;\n  /** ISO-8601 instant the import landed */\n  importedAt: string;\n};\n\n/* --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- the session */\n\n/**\n * An authenticated session, and WHICH PROVIDER ESTABLISHED IT.\n *\n * `provider` is recorded, never simulated. Supabase Auth records the provider that produced a\n * session; how the session was obtained — an email and a password, or a completed Google consent\n * round trip — happens entirely upstream of anything this requirement's leaf ships. AT-001.03's\n * body asserts that `completeSignup` behaves identically for `'google'` and `'email'`, which is a\n * real property of the shipped decision module (it never reads this field). It is NOT a claim that a\n * Google round trip works: see the suite's own note on AT-001.03, and the plan's per-id table.\n *\n * `'github'` joins the two for AT-001.02 and carries exactly the same caveat: a github-established\n * session is the state Auth is in AFTER a consent round trip, and no handshake is simulated to reach\n * it. The establishing provider still participates in NO decision the shipped modules make — the\n * volunteer gate reads a LINKED IDENTITY, which is a different fact (see `linkGithubIdentity`).\n */\nexport type SessionProvider = 'email' | 'google' | 'github';\n\nexport type Session = {\n  /** the auth user id, which is also the `public.accounts` primary key once signup completes */\n  accountId: string;\n  email: string;\n  provider: SessionProvider;\n};\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- the outcomes */\n\nexport type SignInOutcome = { ok: true; session: Session } | { ok: false; reason: string };\n\nexport type CompleteSignupOutcome =\n  | { ok: true; accountId: string; organizationId: string | null }\n  | { ok: false; reason: string };\n\nexport type CreateOrganizationOutcome = { ok: true; organizationId: string } | { ok: false; reason: string };\n\n/**\n * The outcome of an attempted Discovery message — and the refusal carries WHY, for the reason\n * `Decision` in the shipped module gives: AT-001.10 asserts that the block NAMES verification as\n * the remedy, so a bare boolean would make the criterion untestable.\n */\nexport type SendDiscoveryMessageOutcome = { ok: true } | { ok: false; reason: string };\n\n/* ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ the SUT */\n\n/**\n * REQ-001's accounts system under test.\n *\n * The split is the point, and there are now FOUR kinds of member here rather than three:\n *\n *   1. THE TWO PRODUCT OPERATIONS — `completeSignup` and `createOrganization`. At loop tier the\n *      adapter runs them over its own storage but delegates every judgement to\n *      `supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts`, the module the edge functions import; at\n *      integration tier they would be the deployed edge functions.\n *   2. SUPABASE AUTH'S HALF — registration, sign-in, identity linking, and the verification state\n *      (`emailVerified`, `emailedVerificationLink`, `useVerificationLink`). None of it is this\n *      requirement's code. It is what produces the states the product operations judge.\n *   3. READ-BACK — the row shapes an assertion reads after the fact.\n *   4. ONE STAND-IN SURFACE, AND IT IS THE ONLY ONE — `sendDiscoveryMessage`, with\n *      `discoveryMessagesBy` reading it back.\n *\n * THE FOURTH KIND NEEDS ITS OWN SENTENCE, because it is the one that could be misread. NO\n * DISCOVERY ROUTE EXISTS IN THIS TREE. The Discovery message route is REQ-002/004's, and what\n * this requirement's leaf ships is the DECISION that route must consult —\n * `discoveryMessageAllowed` in `supabase/functions/_shared/verification.ts`. So\n * `sendDiscoveryMessage` is not a deployed operation being tested; it is a stand-in surface whose\n * only job is to put the shipped decision on a tested path, exactly as\n * `hasPlatformAcknowledgment` below is the hook a project-creation leaf must call. A green over\n * it says the DECISION is right. It says nothing about enforcement anywhere, because there is\n * nowhere yet to enforce it.\n */\nexport type AccountsSut = {\n  /** AT-001.07's second clause: which types the PUBLIC signup surface offers. */\n  publicSignupAccountTypes(): Promise<readonly string[]>;\n\n  /* --- Supabase Auth's half. Not this leaf's code; it is what produces a session to complete. --- */\n\n  /**\n   * Register an auth user with an email and a password, and return the resulting session.\n   *\n   * IT STILL RETURNS A SESSION, AND THE HONEST GAP THAT LEAVES IS STATED HERE RATHER THAN HIDDEN.\n   * With `enable_confirmations = true` the live GoTrue issues NO session at signup, so on the real\n   * stack a completed-but-unverified account is not reachable by the public path. This fixture's\n   * `Session` has always been an identity handle rather than an access token, and session ISSUANCE\n   * is D2.L2's subject (AT-001.12, .13 — declared red). So at the loop tier such an account IS\n   * constructible, and AT-001.10's body constructs one deliberately: the gate exists because\n   * decision-8 makes verification the write path's own floor, not a property borrowed from the\n   * session layer. The live-stack behaviour is measured in `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts`\n   * checks (a) and (c), and nowhere else in this item.\n   */\n  registerWithEmailPassword(email: string, password: string): Promise<Session>;\n  /**\n   * A session Auth recorded as having come from an external provider.\n   *\n   * It performs NO handshake and fabricates no authorization code or token exchange: it is the state\n   * Auth is in AFTER a consent round trip, which is the only part of the story any agent can reach.\n   */\n  registerWithProvider(provider: SessionProvider, email: string): Promise<Session>;\n  /**\n   * A GitHub OAuth signup, as Auth records it: a session established by GitHub whose account already\n   * carries the linked GitHub identity — AT-001.02's \"signs up via GitHub OAuth\".\n   *\n   * Like `registerWithProvider` it performs NO handshake. It is `registerWithProvider('github', …)`\n   * plus the fact that a GitHub signup necessarily links the GitHub identity it signed up with, and\n   * it is a separate member rather than an argument because that second half is what AT-001.02's\n   * \"the GitHub identity is linked to it\" clause is about.\n   */\n  registerWithGithub(email: string, githubHandle: string): Promise<Session>;\n  /**\n   * The state Auth is in after a `linkIdentity` round trip — AT-001.04's \"linking completes signup\".\n   *\n   * A LINKED IDENTITY IS NOT THE ESTABLISHING PROVIDER, and keeping the two apart is the point of\n   * this member existing at all: an email- or Google-established volunteer links GitHub and their\n   * session's `provider` does not change. The gate in\n   * `supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts` reads the link; nothing shipped reads the provider.\n   *\n   * Same posture as every other Auth member here — no handshake, no fabricated authorization code.\n   */\n  linkGithubIdentity(session: Session, githubHandle: string): Promise<void>;\n  /** Return sign-in with the same credentials — AT-001.01's final clause. */\n  signInWithEmailPassword(email: string, password: string): Promise<SignInOutcome>;\n  /**\n   * The return visit through an external provider — AT-001.02's \"a later sign-in via GitHub returns\n   * to the same account\".\n   *\n   * What it asserts is IDENTITY CONTINUITY, which is the half of that clause reachable without a\n   * person: an existing user whose account carries that provider's identity signs in to the SAME\n   * account rather than to a second one. The consent round trip itself is not performed and is named\n   * unproved in the plan's per-id table.\n   */\n  signInWithProvider(provider: SessionProvider, email: string): Promise<SignInOutcome>;\n\n  /* ------------------------- Supabase Auth's verification state ------------------------------- */\n\n  /**\n   * Whether Supabase Auth reports this account's email address confirmed — AT-001.09's observable.\n   *\n   * IT IS DERIVED, NOT STORED-AND-READ. The adapter renders the account's stored Auth state as the\n   * canonical GoTrue `/auth/v1/user` shape and judges the answer out of it with the SHIPPED\n   * `emailVerifiedFromUser`. That is the same pattern `completeSignup` uses for the GitHub handle\n   * (the GitHub leaf's gate-2 ruling R3), and it exists so the shipped extractor sits on the tested\n   * path rather than being a function no test ever runs — which is exactly how the future Discovery\n   * route will resolve the same fact from the same response.\n   *\n   * WHAT NO LOOP-TIER GREEN OVER IT PROVES: that the rendered shape is the shape GoTrue really\n   * sends. Only `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts` check (d) touches that, by feeding a real\n   * response to the same shipped function.\n   */\n  emailVerified(accountId: string): Promise<boolean>;\n  /**\n   * The verification link emailed to this address, or `null` when none was emailed.\n   *\n   * `null` is a real answer rather than only the empty case: a provider registration is confirmed\n   * by the provider and no confirmation email is sent for it.\n   */\n  emailedVerificationLink(email: string): Promise<string | null>;\n  /**\n   * Use a verification link. Using the link Auth emailed for an address confirms that address; a\n   * link that was never issued returns `ok: false` and flips nothing.\n   *\n   * NO EXPIRY, NO SINGLE USE AND NO RESEND IS MODELLED OR ASSERTED. AT-001.11 is retired — the\n   * acceptance file's own words are that \"verification-link expiry/single-use/resend semantics are\n   * not stated in REQ-001\" — so building any of them here would be asserting a criterion that was\n   * deliberately withdrawn.\n   *\n   * THE NEVER-ISSUED NEGATIVE IS NOT AT-001.11 GROUND, and the difference is worth being exact\n   * about. It is not a claim about a link's lifetime; it guards this test's own oracle. A\n   * link-shaped string that verified an account it never belonged to would make \"using the emailed\n   * link is what flips it\" mean nothing at all, because any string would flip anything.\n   */\n  useVerificationLink(link: string): Promise<{ ok: boolean }>;\n\n  /* ----------------------------------- the two product operations ----------------------------- */\n\n  /** `supabase/functions/complete-signup` — turns an authenticated auth user into a typed account. */\n  completeSignup(session: Session, request: CompleteSignupRequest, ip: string): Promise<CompleteSignupOutcome>;\n  /** `supabase/functions/create-organization` — the NGO-only action AT-001.06 drives. */\n  createOrganization(session: Session, organizationName: string): Promise<CreateOrganizationOutcome>;\n\n  /* --------------------------- the Discovery gate's stand-in surface -------------------------- */\n\n  /**\n   * Attempt to send a Discovery message — AT-001.10's action.\n   *\n   * THERE IS NO DEPLOYED ROUTE BEHIND THIS, at either tier. See the fourth kind in this type's own\n   * header: the Discovery route is REQ-002/004's and does not exist, so this member is a stand-in\n   * surface that exists to put the SHIPPED `discoveryMessageAllowed` on a tested path. At loop\n   * tier the adapter derives the caller's verified fact through the shipped\n   * `emailVerifiedFromUser`, consults the shipped gate, and refuses with the GATE's own reason.\n   *\n   * THE BODY IS AN OPAQUE STRING AND NOTHING ELSE. Recipients, threads, attachments and message\n   * state are REQ-002/004's semantics; inventing any of them here to make this member look real\n   * would be building another requirement's surface early. What AT-001.10 needs is an attempted\n   * write and a way to see whether it happened, and one string supplies both.\n   */\n  sendDiscoveryMessage(session: Session, body: string): Promise<SendDiscoveryMessageOutcome>;\n  /**\n   * The Discovery message bodies this account has sent — read-back, and it is not optional.\n   *\n   * A BLOCK WHOSE WRITE HAPPENED ANYWAY IS NOT A BLOCK. The refusal's own return value cannot show\n   * that nothing was written, for the same reason `organizationsNamed` exists: a refusal hands\n   * back no identifier, so the row that must NOT be there can only be looked for by who would have\n   * written it.\n   */\n  discoveryMessagesBy(accountId: string): Promise<string[]>;\n\n  /* ------------------------------------------- read-back -------------------------------------- */\n\n  account(accountId: string): Promise<AccountRow | null>;\n  organization(organizationId: string): Promise<OrganizationRow | null>;\n  membership(organizationId: string, accountId: string): Promise<MembershipRow | null>;\n  acknowledgments(accountId: string): Promise<AcknowledgmentRow[]>;\n  /**\n   * The volunteer's imported GitHub profile, or `null` — AT-001.05's observable.\n   *\n   * `null` is a real and asserted answer, not merely the empty case: AT-001.05's pre-completion\n   * negative reads it AFTER the identity is linked and BEFORE the signup completes, and requires\n   * null there. That is what proves the population is CAUSED by completion rather than sitting in a\n   * queue somewhere waiting to be drained.\n   */\n  volunteerProfile(accountId: string): Promise<VolunteerProfileRow | null>;\n\n  /**\n   * The two read-backs a REFUSED action needs, which the keyed ones above cannot serve.\n   *\n   * Asserting that a rejection wrote nothing means looking for rows whose identifiers were never\n   * handed out — a refused `createOrganization` returns no organisation id. So one search by the\n   * name that was attempted, and one listing of everything an account is a member of. Without them\n   * the strongest statement available is \"the call said no\", and an implementation that writes both\n   * rows and then says no would satisfy it.\n   */\n  organizationsNamed(name: string): Promise<OrganizationRow[]>;\n  membershipsOf(accountId: string): Promise<MembershipRow[]>;\n  /**\n   * `public.has_platform_acknowledgment(account_id)` — the observable form of AT-001.01's \"before\n   * any project creation is possible\".\n   *\n   * It MUST DISCRIMINATE: false for an authenticated user who has not completed signup, true after.\n   * The body asserts both halves for exactly that reason.\n   *\n   * WHICH IMPLEMENTATION THAT ASSERTION REACHES depends on the tier, and the difference matters. At\n   * loop tier it reaches the fixture adapter's storage query, so what goes green is the RULE and the\n   * adapter's storage — the shipped SQL function could return true unconditionally and the loop tier\n   * would not notice. `public.has_platform_acknowledgment` itself is proved by step 7(h) of the\n   * plan, against the live database, and by nothing else in this item.\n   *\n   * What this leaf cannot do at either tier is ENFORCE the clause — nothing in the tree creates a\n   * project, and building project creation belongs to another requirement. This is the hook the leaf\n   * that lands project creation must call.\n   */\n  hasPlatformAcknowledgment(accountId: string): Promise<boolean>;\n\n  /* ------------------------------------ the provisioned admin --------------------------------- */\n\n  /**\n   * Provision a `platform_admin` — the ONLY legal way one exists, because the public path refuses\n   * the type. It sits apart from `completeSignup` rather than being one of its options for that\n   * reason.\n   *\n   * ON THE LIVE STACK THIS IS **NOT** A SERVICE-ROLE WRITE, and the correction matters more than\n   * most: this comment used to say it was, and a later reader following it would have granted the\n   * service role an INSERT on `public.accounts` — a write path straight past `complete_signup`'s\n   * `platform_admin` refusal, which is the one guard that sits on the only write path. The service\n   * role holds no INSERT anywhere in this schema, by measurement and by decision. Provisioning is a\n   * direct database operation by an operator: a NARROWER authority than the service role, not a\n   * wider one, and not one any running service holds.\n   */\n  provisionPlatformAdmin(email: string, password: string): Promise<Session>;\n};\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- the world */\n\n/**\n * What REQ-001's fixture world adds to the shared seam.\n *\n * An INTERSECTION, not `interface World extends WorldSeam`, for the alias reason above.\n *\n * One member, and it earns its place: every test in this suite registers auth users, and two tests\n * that happened to pick the same address would interfere in a way that looks like a product defect.\n * `email()` namespaces an address to the world that issued it, so isolation is structural rather\n * than a convention each body has to remember.\n */\nexport type World = WorldSeam & {\n  /** a mail address unique to THIS world, e.g. `email('ngo')` */\n  email(local: string): string;\n};","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":398,"totalLines":398,"truncated":false}},"title":"tests\\at\\suites\\req-001\\_contract.ts","time":{"start":1786290444173,"end":1786290444180}},"id":"prt_fe73516700016zNhGScdblPGXC","sessionID":"ses_018cbdef8ffeIiDUqdR6dh2IlP","messageID":"msg_fe734f578001LZ7j7nF32EROiX"}}
